// Arm64LinuxRuntime — the arm64-LINUX OS boundary: the AArch64 syscall table this backend reaches the kernel
// through, and the hand-assembled HOST SURFACE (`mrt_host_*`) standing behind the filesystem, directory,
// standard-input, CLOCK, executable-path, processor-count and GREEN-THREAD ops on a raw static image with no
// libc. The Linux twin of `Arm64DarwinRuntime`, and the arm64 twin of `X64LinuxRuntime`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **L4 ADDED THE SCHEDULER'S HOST OBJECTS, AND THEY SHARE NO PRIMITIVE WITH THE DARWIN TWIN'S.** That
// file builds a wake event out of a `pthread_mutex_t` and a `pthread_cond_t`, a lock out of a recursive
// pthread mutex, and a per-thread slot out of a pthread key. None of the three exists here; all three are
// `futex` and `TPIDR_EL0` instead, which is a SMALLER surface rather than a harder one — see the L4 banner
// below for why one kernel primitive replaces three library ones.
//
// ⭐ **L3 ADDED FOUR CHUNKS THAT ARE NOT FILESYSTEM WORK AT ALL** — the wall clock, the per-thread CPU clock,
// `/proc/self/exe` and the processor count — and they sit under their own banners below. Each is here for
// the SAME structural reason the filesystem chunks are (a caller's out-param pointer, or a buffer, that must
// outlive a call), and none of them touches the errno word: nothing consumes an `osLastError` after any of
// the four.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THIS LANE HAS THAT DARWIN'S DOES NOT: NOTHING TO CALL.** Darwin's surface is libSystem
// sequences — `opendir`, `readdir`, `fnmatch`, `malloc`, `getcwd`, `__error()` — bound by dyld through a GOT.
// A static ELF links none of that, so every one of those becomes either a raw `svc #0` or code written out
// here. Three consequences shape the whole file, and each is stated where it bites:
//
//   • **THERE IS NO `errno`.** A `svc` answers `-errno` in x0 and nothing stores it anywhere. `osLastError`
//     is a SEPARATE op emitted after the failing one (`ManagedFileRuntime.emitWin32BoolOutcome` states that
//     ordering), so every chunk here STASHES the reason where `mrt_host_last_error` reads it back —
//     this OS thread's own slot when it has one and a `.data` word (`LinuxErrnoLabel`) before it does,
//     which `arm64LinuxEmitErrnoAddr` decides at every site. That pair is this lane's `*__error()`, and it
//     is per-thread for the same reason Darwin's is.
//   • **THERE IS NO `fnmatch`.** `FindFirstFileA`'s glob is matched by `mrt_linux_glob_match`, written out
//     below. It is the one place the Darwin surface's shape could not be reused even in outline.
//   • **THERE IS NO `malloc`.** The search object `osFileFindFirst` hands back is ONE `mmap`'d page holding
//     the descriptor, the pattern, the directory-prefix scratch and the `getdents64` buffer, released by one
//     `munmap`. Darwin's is a 16-byte `malloc` plus an `opendir` handle plus a duplicated pattern.
//
// ⭐ **EVERYTHING ELSE IS SHARED, AND THREE FILES ARE WHERE.** The symbol names and the POSIX constants are
// `Targets/Shared/PosixRuntime`; the Linux facts both arches agree on are `Targets/Shared/LinuxRuntime`; the
// AArch64 emit fragments and the chunks whose body is one shape on both arm64 lanes — the errno→Win32
// mapping, the `dwFileAttributes` a `st_mode` becomes, the `WIN32_FIND_DATAA` fill and the two string walkers
// behind the glob split — are `Arm64PosixRuntime`. Only `struct stat`'s layout differs, and that is a
// parameter.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY CHUNK HERE IS LINUX-ONLY AND IS APPENDED ONLY FOR `Os.linux`** (`appendArm64LinuxRuntimeChunks`,
// called from `buildArm64Backend`), under the SAME `RuntimeUsage` bits the Std-tier installers read — so a
// chunk can never be absent for a body that calls it: one bit, two tiers, exactly as the Darwin surface is
// gated.

// --- Linux syscall numbers (AArch64) ---
//
// The AArch64 table, NOT x86-64's: `write` is 64 here and 1 there, `mmap` 222 and 9. Named because
// a bare 64 at a call site is unreadable and the number IS the primitive's identity.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE `Arm64` PREFIX IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT DECORATION — IT WAS THE BUG** (found by A1y's review).
// These three were spelled `LinuxSyscallWrite`/`LinuxSyscallMmap`/`LinuxSyscallExitGroup`, and
// `Targets/X64/X64LinuxRuntime` exported THE SAME THREE NAMES over the x86-64 numbers. Maxon's top-level
// name space is FLAT: a declaration's directory grants it nothing, `export` publishes to the whole
// program, and two exported globals of one name are resolved SILENTLY BY FILE ORDER with no diagnostic
// (probed against the bootstrap — the reader's own directory is irrelevant, and the winner follows
// POSITION, not value). x64's file came last, so every CROSS-FILE read lost, including this backend's.
//
// MEASURED on arm64-linux, by disassembling every `svc #0` in a cross-compiled binary:
// `StdToArm64Conversion.lowerOsExit`/`lowerPanic` emitted **231**, `lowerOsWrite` **1** and
// `lowerOsMmap` **9** — the x86-64 numbers. On AArch64 those are `munlockall`, `io_destroy` and
// `lgetxattr`: none exits, writes or maps, and none fails loudly, so a violated range guard did not
// abort at all — it fell through to `mov x0, xzr; ret` and the program carried on with a zero. The ONE
// use that was right is the entry stub's below, because a file's own declaration shadows the import.
//
// ⭐ The committed `specs-shv2/fragments/arm64-linux/**` goldens record `arm64.syscall 94` at all 97
// sites, so this restores agreement with them rather than moving them — and their disagreement with what
// this compiler emitted was the tell. ⚠ THOSE GOLDENS WERE PRODUCED ON THE arm64 HOST, AFTER the
// collision existed, which means the losing declaration was not even the SAME one on both machines: file
// order is a property of the CHECKOUT, so a name collision makes the emitted code depend on which box
// compiled the compiler. That is the strongest reason the cure is a name and not a comment, and it is why
// this must never be "left for the lane that can run it" — nothing about it is arm64-specific but the
// numbers. The comment two paragraphs up said "NOT x86-64's" the whole time,
// which is the project's cure ladder (DERIVE > CHECK > COMMENT) failing at its bottom rung: a comment
// cannot make a name resolve. One name per fact can.
//
// ⚠ `exit_group` (94), NOT `exit` (93) — a DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE from v1, which emits 93. `exit`
// terminates the calling THREAD; `exit_group` terminates the whole thread group, which is what a C
// `_exit` actually invokes and what `main` returning means. ⛔ **THIS USED TO READ *"the two are
// indistinguishable in a single-threaded process, so this costs nothing today and stays correct the day
// shv2 grows OS threads (P1.5)"*, AND THAT DAY HAS COME ON THIS LANE**: `osThreadCreate` here is a real
// `clone` (`StdToArm64Conversion.lowerOsThreadCreate`), so a green-thread program runs worker Ms and the
// divergence is load-bearing rather than free — 93 would end the calling thread and leave the others
// running.
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallWrite = 64
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallMmap = 222
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallExitGroup = 94

// F1a — the page-LIFECYCLE half of the mapping ABI: `munmap` releases a mapping, `madvise` drops its physical
// backing while keeping the mapping. AArch64's numbers, NOT x86-64's (11 and 28 there — see the twin block in
// `Targets/X64/X64LinuxRuntime.maxon`), which is the whole reason this table carries an arch prefix. There is
// deliberately no recommit number: a `MADV_DONTNEED`ed page refaults on the next touch, so re-backing one is a
// Windows-only call and arm64 emits nothing for it.
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallMunmap = 215
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallMadvise = 233

// F2 — the per-thread identity word. `gettid` answers the calling thread's kernel thread id: per-thread
// stable and never 0, which is exactly `StdOp.currentThreadToken`'s contract. It is asked instead of reading
// `TPIDR_EL0` because shv2's Linux lane is a raw static binary with no libc, so nothing has written that
// register and it reads 0 — the op's own header carries the full argument. 178 on AArch64, 186 on x86-64
// (`X64LinuxRuntime`'s twin), which is exactly why these numbers wear the arch prefix.
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallGettid = 178

// --- The rest of the table: the FILESYSTEM syscalls (MAC4/5/6's Linux twins) ---
//
// ⭐ **THE `*at` FAMILY IS THE ONLY SPELLING AArch64 HAS.** There is no plain `open`, `stat`, `unlink`,
// `rename` or `mkdir` on this architecture — the kernel exposes only the directory-relative forms, so every
// path operation below prepends `AT_FDCWD` and means "relative to the working directory". v1 discovered the
// same thing and says so at its own `renameat` (`maxon-selfhosted/Compiler/Targets/Arm64/Arm64Backend.maxon`,
// `lowerOsFileRenameArm64`); these numbers are read off that file, which has run them on this lane.
// L3 — the four the two non-scheduler clocks, the process's identity and the machine query reach the kernel
// through. ⚠ **MEASURED ON THIS LANE, not transcribed**: each was invoked by number through `syscall(2)` on
// an aarch64 Ubuntu 26.04 guest and checked against the libc call it is supposed to be — `getpid` against
// `os.getpid()`, `readlinkat` against the path it answered, both `clock_gettime` clock ids against a real
// calendar and a real CPU cost, and `sched_getaffinity`'s popcount against `nproc`. That is the discipline
// `Arm64LinuxOpenDirectoryFlags` below exists to enforce: v1's own table has `O_DIRECTORY` wrong for this exact
// architecture, so an arm64 number is checked against arm64 or it is a guess.
//
// ⚠ **`clock_gettime` IS 113 AND IS THE SAME NUMBER FOR BOTH CLOCK IDS** — it is the `clockid_t` argument
// that differs, and those are Linux's own values, not Darwin's (see `LinuxClockRealtime`).
let Arm64LinuxSyscallReadlinkat = 78
let Arm64LinuxSyscallClockGettime = 113
let Arm64LinuxSyscallSchedGetaffinity = 123
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallGetPid = 172

// ⚠ **29 HERE AND 16 ON x86-64** — the two Linux lanes disagree about `ioctl` as they disagree about every
// number in this table, which is why it is declared per arch and only the REQUEST word is shared. VERIFIED
// by invoking it: 29 with `TCGETS` answered 0 on a pty and `-ENOTTY` on a pipe, while syscall 28 with the
// identical arguments answered `-EINVAL` (`madvise`), which is the control that says 29 is really `ioctl`.
let Arm64LinuxSyscallIoctl = 29

let Arm64LinuxSyscallGetcwd = 17
let Arm64LinuxSyscallMkdirat = 34
let Arm64LinuxSyscallUnlinkat = 35
let Arm64LinuxSyscallRenameat = 38
let Arm64LinuxSyscallOpenat = 56
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallClose = 57
let Arm64LinuxSyscallGetdents64 = 61
let Arm64LinuxSyscallRead = 63
let Arm64LinuxSyscallNewfstatat = 79
let Arm64LinuxSyscallFstat = 80
let Arm64LinuxSyscallFsync = 82

// --- L4: the GREEN-THREAD FLOOR's syscalls ---
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED ON THIS LANE, not transcribed** — each was invoked by number through `syscall(2)` on an
// aarch64 Ubuntu guest and checked against the libc call it is supposed to be, which is the discipline
// `Arm64LinuxOpenDirectoryFlags` above exists to enforce.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`clone` NOW SERVES TWO DIFFERENT REQUESTS UNDER ONE NUMBER, AND THE FLAG WORD IS THE WHOLE
// DIFFERENCE.** L5's `clone(SIGCHLD)` (below, with the child-process band) shares NOTHING and is exactly
// `fork`; the worker M's `clone(LinuxCloneWorkerFlags)` shares the address space, the descriptors, the
// filesystem context and the thread group, and is exactly `pthread_create`. They are one syscall with two
// argument shapes, so the number stays with the band that first needed it and the FLAGS carry the meaning.
//
// ⚠ `mprotect` is here rather than beside `mmap` because its one caller is `mrt_host_thread_create`, which
// lays a PROT_NONE guard page under each worker M's stack.
let Arm64LinuxSyscallFutex = 98
let Arm64LinuxSyscallClockNanosleep = 115
let Arm64LinuxSyscallMprotect = 226
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallSetPriority = 140
export let Arm64LinuxSyscallGetPriority = 141

// --- L5: the CHILD-PROCESS band's syscalls ---
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED ON THIS LANE, not transcribed** — each was invoked by number through `syscall(2)` on an
// aarch64 Ubuntu guest and checked against the behaviour it is supposed to have: `pipe2` against a real
// read/write pair and against `FD_CLOEXEC` on the ends it hands back; `clone(SIGCHLD)` against a child that
// wrote down a pipe and exited with a chosen code; `execve` against a `/bin/sh` whose output came back;
// `wait4` against that exit code, against `WNOHANG` on a live child and against the `ECHILD` a SECOND wait
// answers; `kill` against a `SIGKILL` the status then reported; `dup3` against the descriptor it produced and
// the `FD_CLOEXEC` it cleared; `ppoll` against a pipe with nothing in it, with five bytes in it, with its
// writer closed, at EOF, and on a bad descriptor.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AArch64 DROPPED THE LEGACY SPELLINGS OF FIVE OF THESE AND THERE IS NO `fork` AT ALL.** No `fork`, no
// `pipe`, no `dup2`, no `poll`, no `open` — the architecture carries only `clone`, `pipe2`, `dup3`, `ppoll`
// and `openat`. Every one takes an argument the legacy call does not, and two of them INVERT a default
// (`ppoll`'s NULL timeout blocks for ever where `poll`'s 0 does not wait at all; `pipe2` can mark
// close-on-exec where `pipe` cannot). Transliterating the Darwin arm would have been wrong five times.
let Arm64LinuxSyscallDup3 = 24
let Arm64LinuxSyscallPipe2 = 59
let Arm64LinuxSyscallPpoll = 73
let Arm64LinuxSyscallExit = 93
let Arm64LinuxSyscallKill = 129
let Arm64LinuxSyscallSetsid = 157
let Arm64LinuxSyscallClone = 220
let Arm64LinuxSyscallExecve = 221
let Arm64LinuxSyscallWait4 = 260
let Arm64LinuxSyscallChdir = 49

// ⭐ **THE FUTEX OPERATIONS, IN THEIR PRIVATE FORMS.** `FUTEX_WAIT` is 0 and `FUTEX_WAKE` is 1;
// `FUTEX_PRIVATE_FLAG` is 128 and says the word is never shared across processes, which lets the kernel key
// its hash bucket on the mm rather than on the physical page. Every futex in this compiler is in an
// anonymous private mapping or in the program's own `.data`, so the private form is always the right one —
// and it is measurably cheaper, which is why glibc uses it for exactly these objects.
let LinuxFutexWaitPrivate = 128
let LinuxFutexWakePrivate = 129

// ⛔⛔ **THE JOIN's WAIT IS THE *SHARED* `FUTEX_WAIT`, AND THAT IS NOT AN INCONSISTENCY — IT IS THE ONLY
// SPELLING THE KERNEL's OWN WAKE CAN REACH.** A worker M is joined through the `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` word
// (`PosixWaitOffThread`), which the KERNEL zeroes and wakes when the thread ends — and it issues that wake
// with `FLAGS_SHARED`. On an anonymous page a shared key carries `FUT_OFF_MMSHARED` where a private key does
// not, so the two hash to DIFFERENT buckets and a private waiter is never woken.
//
// ⭐ **MEASURED on an aarch64 Ubuntu guest, one C program invoking these syscalls BY NUMBER, one constant
// changed between the two runs:** with
// `FUTEX_WAIT_PRIVATE` the joiner slept through the child's exit and the run was killed at its 20 s bound;
// with this plain `FUTEX_WAIT` it was woken by the FIRST wait and read the cleared word. glibc's
// `pthread_join` passes `LLL_SHARED` for exactly this reason. Every OTHER futex in this file is woken by a
// `FUTEX_WAKE_PRIVATE` this compiler emits, so the private form stays right for all of them.
let LinuxFutexWaitShared = 0

// `FUTEX_WAKE`'s count: ONE waiter. An auto-reset event releases exactly one waiter by its Win32 contract,
// and the mutex below hands the lock to one successor.
let LinuxFutexWakeOne = 1

// A NULL `struct timespec *` — `FUTEX_WAIT` with no timeout, i.e. wait until woken.
let LinuxFutexNoTimeout = 0

// `futex(uaddr, op, val, timeout, uaddr2, val3)`. The last two are meaningless for WAIT and WAKE and MUST
// still be passed: a `svc` takes six registers whatever the operation reads, and leaving one holding a stale
// value is how a future requeue-shaped operation would silently pick it up.
let LinuxFutexUnusedArg = 0

// ⭐⭐ **THE VALUE A `FUTEX_WAIT` EXPECTS, AND WHY IT IS THE UNSIGNALLED STATE RATHER THAN THE SIGNALLED ONE.**
// `FUTEX_WAIT(uaddr, val)` sleeps only while `*uaddr == val`, and returns `-EAGAIN` immediately otherwise.
// So the caller passes the state it is waiting to LEAVE: an event waiter passes `PosixWaitNotSignalled`, and
// a signal that lands between its own test and this call makes the syscall return at once rather than sleep
// through it. That is the whole lost-wakeup argument, and it is the kernel's rather than this file's.
//
// ⚠ The two values themselves are `Arm64PosixRuntime`'s, because the WORD is: the signal field sits at the
// same offset on both POSIX lanes and a shared body (`mrt_host_proc_reap`) publishes into it.

// ⭐⭐ **THE WAIT OBJECT ON THIS LANE — ONE `mmap`'d PAGE, AND `Arm64PosixRuntime` OWNS EVERY FIELD IN IT.**
// Darwin's twin carries a `pthread_mutex_t` and a `pthread_cond_t` ABOVE the shared prefix, because a
// condvar needs a mutex to be correct; a futex needs NEITHER — the compare-and-sleep is atomic inside the
// kernel, which is the property the whole primitive exists to provide. So this object is exactly the six
// shared words (`PosixWaitOffKind` … `PosixWaitOffChildKilled`), of which an EVENT uses two, a CHILD PROCESS
// four and a WORKER M's THREAD two — the kind and the `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` join word at
// `PosixWaitOffThread`, which is the slot Darwin fills with a `pthread_t` for the same purpose.
//
// ⚠ **THE SIGNAL WORD IS A 64-BIT SLOT AND THE KERNEL READS ITS LOW 4 BYTES.** A futex is a `u32`; AArch64
// is little-endian, so the low half of an 8-byte aligned word IS that `u32` at the same address. Keeping the
// slot 64 bits lets the publish and the take go through `arm64EmitAtomicCas`, this backend's ONE LL/SC pair,
// instead of a second 32-bit spelling of it — and the value only ever holds 0 or 1, so no bit the kernel
// cannot see is ever set.
//
// ⚠ **`mmap` RATHER THAN A `malloc` THIS LANE DOES NOT HAVE**, and rather than the slab, for the Darwin
// twin's reason: the slab is Std-tier and reachable only through a `bl` the Std reachability walk cannot
// see. A whole page for two words is the mapping granularity, not a choice, and these objects are
// process-lifetime — one per P. ⛔ That last clause used to read *"one per P, and `DefaultMaxProcs` is
// 1"*; there is no such constant any more and the count is the machine's processors, so the waste is a
// page per processor decided once at bring-up rather than the single page it was — still a fixed set,
// still not a per-operation cost.
//
// ⚠ **THE PAGE COMES BACK ZERO-FILLED**, which `MAP_ANONYMOUS` guarantees, so the signal word needs no
// explicit store — unlike Darwin's, whose `malloc` does not zero and which therefore writes every field.
// ⭐ **ONE PAGE, AND IT IS THE SAME PAGE FOR BOTH HOST OBJECTS THIS FILE MAPS** — a wait object and an OS
// thread's slot block. Named once because `mmap`'s granularity is what decides it, not either object's
// contents: a wait object uses two words of it and a slot block as many as the scheduler asks for.
let LinuxHostObjectPageBytes = 4096

// ⭐⭐ **THE RECURSIVE FUTEX MUTEX `osLockInit` BUILDS INTO THE CALLER'S REGION.**
//
// ⛔ **RECURSIVE IS NOT OPTIONAL AND IT WAS MEASURED AS A HANG ON THE OTHER LANE.** A Win32
// `CRITICAL_SECTION` is re-entrant by its owning thread and `SchedRuntime` relies on it in so many words
// (*"a nested `LeaveCriticalSection` releases nothing"*); the first arm64-macOS `async` build hung in
// `__slab_refill` on a NORMAL pthread mutex taken twice down one call chain
// (`Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinLockInitChunk` records it). So the same op must mean the same thing
// here, and a raw futex is not re-entrant — the owner and the depth beside it are what make it so.
//
// The three words: a futex at offset 0 (0 = free, 1 = held with no waiter, 2 = held and someone is asleep on
// it), the owning thread's `gettid`, and how many times that owner has taken it.
//
// ⚠ **THE OWNER WORD IS READ WITHOUT SYNCHRONISATION AND THAT IS SOUND**, for a reason that does not
// generalise: the only value a thread can act on is ITS OWN tid, and no other thread can write that there —
// a non-owner reads 0 or somebody else's tid, and both send it to the acquire path.
let LinuxLockOffFutex = 0x00 as MachineWord
let LinuxLockOffOwner = 0x08 as MachineWord
let LinuxLockOffDepth = 0x10 as MachineWord

// The three states of the futex word above, named because a bare 2 at a `cas` site says nothing about which
// of this file's several 2s it is.
let LinuxMutexFree = 0
let LinuxMutexHeld = 1
let LinuxMutexHeldContended = 2

// The depth an outermost acquire records, and the value `atomicRmw`'s DISPLACED word carries when the
// release was the last one and nobody was asleep.
let LinuxMutexFirstEntry = 1

// `atomicRmw(add)` with this operand is the release decrement: it answers the word it DISPLACED, which is
// what says whether a waiter has to be woken (`LinuxMutexHeldContended`) or not (`LinuxMutexHeld`).
let LinuxMutexReleaseStep = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF

// ⭐⭐ **THIS LANE'S PER-OS-THREAD SLOT BLOCK, AND `TPIDR_EL0` IS ITS BASE.** A static image links no libc, so
// there is no `pthread_getspecific` to call and no TSD array to index — and by the same token NOTHING ELSE
// READS OR WRITES THE WRITABLE THREAD POINTER, which makes it free for the scheduler to define. One page per
// OS thread, `mmap`'d by `mrt_host_tls_alloc` the first time that thread asks for a slot.
//
// ⚠ **KEY 0 IS NEVER HANDED OUT, EXACTLY AS ON DARWIN, AND FOR THE SAME REASON.** The Std tier publishes
// `key * 8 + tlsSlotArrayBase(target)` and every reader treats a ZERO offset as *"`__sched_init_procs` has
// not run"*; the base is 0 here, so a key of 0 would make that offset 0 and every current-P read in the
// program would answer NULL for ever — a scheduler that silently never runs. The counter below therefore
// pre-increments, and the chunk asserts the result rather than trusting the arithmetic.
// How many keys one block holds — DERIVED from the page and the slot width, and a FUNCTION rather than a
// `let` for `runtimeFrameSlots`' language reason: a global initializer folds `/` as a float.
function linuxTlsSlotCount() returns ParsedInt
	return LinuxHostObjectPageBytes / SpillSlotBytes
end 'linuxTlsSlotCount'

// The first key handed out, and the reason the counter pre-increments — see the block above.
//
// ⭐ **SLOT 0 IS NOT MERELY UNISSUED, IT IS THIS THREAD's `errno`** (`arm64LinuxEmitErrnoAddr`). The two
// reasons stack rather than compete: a key of 0 would publish a slot offset of 0, which every current-P
// reader treats as *"the scheduler has not started"*, and the word that offset addresses is already spoken
// for.
let LinuxTlsFirstKey = 1

// ⛔⛔ **`O_DIRECTORY` IS 0o40000 ON AArch64, NOT THE 0o200000 EVERY asm-generic TABLE SHOWS.**
// `arch/arm64/include/uapi/asm/fcntl.h` OVERRIDES four flags before including the generic header — for
// AArch32 compat — and shifts them all down: `O_DIRECTORY` 040000, `O_NOFOLLOW` 0100000, `O_DIRECT` 0200000,
// `O_LARGEFILE` 0400000. So the generic 0200000 this was first written as is arm64's **O_DIRECT**, and the
// three flags above are unaffected because arm64 overrides none of THEM — which is exactly why every file
// open worked while the directory open did not.
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED, AND IT FAILED IN THE WORST-SHAPED WAY**: `openat(AT_FDCWD, "probe_dir", 0o200000)` answered
// **-EINVAL**, an errno with no Win32 twin, so every `Directory.list` and every glob `openSearch` reported
// the catch-all `openSearchFailed` instead of a real reason — while `__mf_exists`/`__mf_stat`, which take the
// single-`stat` route and never open a directory, passed. ⚠ v1's own flag-translation comment
// (`emitMacosOpenFlagXlateArm64`) records `O_DIRECTORY 0x10000` for this very architecture. It is wrong, and
// it is wrong in the one file this port reads its syscall numbers from.
let Arm64LinuxOpenDirectoryFlags = 0o40000

// `ENOTSUP` (`EOPNOTSUPP`) — 95 on Linux and 45 on Darwin, so it is per-lane for `LinuxErrnoNameTooLong`'s
// reason and cannot join the five in `Targets/Shared/PosixRuntime`. Like `Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s twin it is PUBLISHED
// rather than read: `mrt_host_spawn` answers it for a caller-built environment block, a shape this lane has
// no conversion for either. See that chunk's own note.
let LinuxErrnoNotSupported = 95

// ==========================================================================
// The errno word
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THIS LANE'S `errno`, AND IT HAS TO BE SOMEWHERE.** `StdOp.osLastError` is emitted as a SEPARATE op
// immediately after the call that failed, so the reason must survive from one to the other; a `svc` reports
// it in x0 and keeps no copy. libc solves this with thread-local storage, and so does this lane — the
// difference is only that the storage is the scheduler's own slot block rather than a `__thread` the linker
// laid out, plus the `.data` word below for a thread that has no block.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE WORD WENT PER-M THE DAY THIS LANE GREW A REAL `clone`, WHICH IS THE DAY ITS OWN NOTE SAID IT
// WOULD HAVE TO.** That note read: *"THE OBLIGATION IS THEREFORE RE-POINTED, NOT DISCHARGED: the day this
// lane starts an OS THREAD, this slot must go per-M … that day is the day `osThreadCreate` grows a real
// `clone`, and the abort there is what will announce it."* The abort announced it, and this is the fix
// rather than a third re-pointing: with two Ms making syscalls at once, a `-errno` stashed by one and read
// by `mrt_host_last_error` on the other is a WRONG `FileError` — a silent wrong answer, not a crash, on a
// lane where nothing would notice.
//
// ⇒ **`arm64LinuxEmitErrnoAddr` IS THE ADDRESS EVERY READER AND EVERY WRITER GOES THROUGH**, and it answers
// this OS thread's own slot whenever the thread has one. This `.data` word is what is left: the storage for
// a thread that has never asked the scheduler for a slot block, which is every thread of a program with no
// green threads in it, and the main thread of a green-threaded one up to `__sched_init_procs`.
//
// ⇒ **AND ITS SHARING CLASS FOLLOWS THE FIX.** It was `lastWriterWins` — one word whose contract never said
// which writer wins — and it is `sealedBeforeWorkers` now, which is stronger and is true by construction:
// once `mrt_host_tls_alloc` has published a block for a thread, that thread never writes here again, and
// `__sched_init_procs` (which allocates the scheduler's key) is the same bring-up no worker M can race.

// ⭐ **THE THREE VERBS ONLY THIS LANE SPELLS AS A CHUNK, SO THEY KEEP THE LANE'S INFIX.**
// `Targets/Shared/PosixRuntime`'s header states the rule: a name goes under the shared `mrt_host_` prefix
// when the VERB is a shared host-surface verb served by every lane. These three are not — on macOS `osLockEnter` and
// `osLockLeave` are a bare `pthread_mutex_lock`/`_unlock` import with no chunk at all, and `tlsSetValue` is
// one `pthread_setspecific`. Only a raw static image needs a body, and a body one lane builds wears that
// lane's name, exactly as `mrt_linux_glob_match` does.
export let LinuxLockEnterName = b"mrt_linux_lock_enter"
export let LinuxLockLeaveName = b"mrt_linux_lock_leave"
export let LinuxTlsSetName = b"mrt_linux_tls_set"

// The per-OS-thread slot counter `mrt_host_tls_alloc` hands out of. (The environment vector's word is
// `LinuxRuntime.LinuxEnvpLabel` — both lanes capture it, so it is not this one's to declare.)
let LinuxTlsNextKeyLabel = b"__mrt_tls_next_key"

// The `.data` slot this lane lays out. Gated on the FACILITIES that stash into it rather than on the target
// alone: a Linux program that touches no file, no directory and no standard input never writes the word, so
// it does not carry it.
//
// ⚠ **NO `os` TEST INSIDE THE `usage` TEST — the target is asked FIRST**, because the answer really does
// depend on both and this is the one place that pair is stated (`commandLineRuntimeGlobals`' arrangement).
export function arm64LinuxRuntimeGlobals(usage RuntimeUsage, target Target) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	if target.cpu != CpuArch.arm64 or target.os != Os.linux 'notThisLane'
		return out
	end 'notThisLane'

	if not arm64LinuxTouchesErrnoWord(usage) 'noHostSurface'
		return out
	end 'noHostSurface'

	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: LinuxErrnoLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	return out
end 'arm64LinuxRuntimeGlobals'

// ⭐ **THE GREEN-THREAD FLOOR'S `.data` WORD, ON THE FLOOR'S OWN BIT.** Split from the errno word above
// rather than folded into it because the two conditions are genuinely different: a program that reads a file
// carries the errno word and no scheduler, and a pure-`async` program carries this one and no errno word.
//
// ⚠ **NO `os` TEST INSIDE THE `usage` TEST — the target is asked FIRST**, exactly as its neighbour does it.
export function arm64LinuxGreenThreadGlobals(usage RuntimeUsage, target Target) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	if target.cpu != CpuArch.arm64 or target.os != Os.linux or not usage.usesGt 'notThisLane'
		return out
	end 'notThisLane'

	// The per-OS-thread slot counter. `__sched_init_procs` is its only caller and runs during bring-up, which
	// is what makes the plain read-modify-write in `mrt_host_tls_alloc` sound.
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: LinuxTlsNextKeyLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers)
	return out
end 'arm64LinuxGreenThreadGlobals'

// ⭐ **WHO READS THE errno WORD — the union of the facilities that emit `StdOp.osLastError`, and therefore
// the gate on `mrt_host_last_error` itself.** `usesIocp` joined it at L5: `__gt_subp_yield_read` classifies a
// failed pipe read with it, and a spawn failure is reported through it.
function arm64LinuxUsesHostSurface(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return usage.usesManagedFile or usage.usesManagedDirectory or usage.usesConsoleStdin or usage.usesIocp
end 'arm64LinuxUsesHostSurface'

// ⭐⭐ **WHO TOUCHES IT AT ALL — the readers above, PLUS the bodies that only ever STASH.** It is DERIVED from
// its own subset rather than restated, so the `.data` layout cannot come apart from the chunk list.
//
// ⛔ **THE STASH-ONLY PRODUCER IS THE SPAWN, AND IT RIDES `usesGt` RATHER THAN ANY HOST-SURFACE BIT.**
// `installGtRuntime` builds `__gt_process_run` unconditionally, so `mrt_host_spawn` — whose failure arm
// stashes an errno like every other body here — is laid out in every green-thread program, including one
// that reads no pipe and opens no file. MEASURED: a `runProcess("exit 3")` that discards its child's output
// sets `usesGt` and NOTHING else, and gating the word on the reader union alone answered
// `GlobalDataTable.dataSectionOffsetOf: no .data slot is labelled '__mrt_errno'` at BACKEND time — which is
// a panic and not a diagnostic.
function arm64LinuxTouchesErrnoWord(usage RuntimeUsage) returns bool
	return arm64LinuxUsesHostSurface(usage) or usage.usesGt
end 'arm64LinuxTouchesErrnoWord'

// ==========================================================================
// struct stat (asm-generic, which AArch64 uses)
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐ **128 BYTES, AND NOT ONE OFFSET AGREES WITH DARWIN'S.** `st_size` is at 48 here and 96 there; `st_mode`
// at 16 here and 4 there. Read off `include/uapi/asm-generic/stat.h`, the definition AArch64 uses.
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO BIRTH TIME.** Linux's `struct stat` carries `st_atime`/`st_mtime`/`st_ctime` and nothing
// else; a creation time is `statx`'s `stx_btime` and needs a different syscall and a different structure. So
// `File.info().createdTime` reports `st_ctime` — the inode's last STATUS CHANGE — on this lane, which is the
// closest thing a `stat` can answer and is what every POSIX port of this surface reports.
//
// ⚠ **v1 WRITES `st_mode` AT 24 AND THAT IS WRONG** (`emitMacosStatRepackArm64`'s destination offsets);
// 24 is `st_uid`. It is unobserved there because the field it repacks into is read back at the same wrong
// offset, so the two errors cancel — which is exactly why these were read off the kernel header rather than
// transcribed from the reference.
let Arm64LinuxStatBytes = 128
let Arm64LinuxStatModeOffset = 16
let Arm64LinuxStatSizeOffset = 48
let Arm64LinuxStatAccessTimeOffset = 72
let Arm64LinuxStatModifyTimeOffset = 88
let Arm64LinuxStatStatusChangeTimeOffset = 104

function arm64LinuxStatLayout() returns PosixStatLayout
	return PosixStatLayout.create(Arm64LinuxStatBytes, modeOffset: Arm64LinuxStatModeOffset, sizeOffset: Arm64LinuxStatSizeOffset, creationTimeOffset: Arm64LinuxStatStatusChangeTimeOffset, accessTimeOffset: Arm64LinuxStatAccessTimeOffset, modifyTimeOffset: Arm64LinuxStatModifyTimeOffset)
end 'arm64LinuxStatLayout'

// ==========================================================================
// L3 — the `timespec` this assembler's clock chunks read into
// ==========================================================================

// The clock IDS are `Targets/Shared/LinuxRuntime`'s: no architecture overrides them, so they are one fact
// for both Linux lanes.

// `struct timespec` — `tv_sec` then `tv_nsec`, two 64-bit words on AArch64, which is exactly two of this
// assembler's 8-byte frame slots side by side. So `&slot` IS a `struct timespec *`, the same identity
// `DarwinTimespecSecSlot` relies on for `nanosleep`.
//
// ⚠ **THE INDICES ARE FIXED HERE, NOT PER CHUNK, BECAUSE `arm64LinuxReadClockTimespec` ADDRESSES THEM.**
// Both clock chunks hold their out-param pointer in slot 0 and reserve these two above it
// (`beginFunction(1 + LinuxTimespecSlots)`), so the fragment can form the address without being told where.
// A chunk that reserved fewer would not be subtly wrong: `Arm64RuntimeAsm.slotOffset` PANICS on a slot
// outside the frame it opened.
let LinuxTimespecSecSlot = 1 as StackSize
let LinuxTimespecNsecSlot = 2 as StackSize

// ==========================================================================
// Shared emit fragments
// ==========================================================================

// The address of one of this lane's `.data` words. x16 (IP0) is the address scratch throughout this file:
// nothing here makes an import call, so the trampoline that normally claims it does not exist on this lane.
function arm64LinuxEmitDataAddr(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, dest Arm64Register, label ByteArray)
	arm64EmitAdrpAddPair(asm.code, rdataRelocs: asm.rdataRelocs, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(dest), label: label, adrpKind: RdataRelocKind.dataSectionArm64Adrp, pageOffKind: RdataRelocKind.dataSectionArm64PageOff)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitDataAddr'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ADDRESS OF *THIS OS THREAD's* errno WORD — the one door every stash and the one read go
// through.** `TPIDR_EL0` is this lane's per-OS-thread slot block (`mrt_host_tls_alloc`), and SLOT 0 of it is
// this word: the scheduler's own keys start at `LinuxTlsFirstKey`, so slot 0 was already never handed out
// and is now RESERVED rather than merely unused. A thread that has no block yet — every thread of a program
// with no scheduler, and the main thread before `__sched_init_procs` — falls back to the process-wide
// `.data` word, which is sound because at that moment it is the only thread there is.
//
// ⚠ **NO INSTRUCTION HERE TOUCHES THE FLAGS**, which is load-bearing: `arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed` branches
// on a `cmp` taken BEFORE the stash and `arm64LinuxEmitFailureSentinel` reads one after it. `mrs`, `cbnz`,
// `adrp` and the `add` of an `adrp`/`add` pair all leave NZCV alone.
function arm64LinuxEmitErrnoAddr(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, dest Arm64Register)
	let perThread = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitThreadPointer(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(dest), source: Arm64ThreadPointerSource.linuxWritable)
	arm64EmitCbnzTo(asm, reg: dest, label: perThread)
	arm64LinuxEmitDataAddr(asm, dest: dest, label: LinuxErrnoLabel)

	asm.placeLabel(perThread)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitErrnoAddr'

// x0 holds a FAILED syscall's `-errno`. Stash the POSITIVE errno where `mrt_host_last_error` reads it, and
// leave that positive value in x9 for a caller that must also test it (`mrt_host_dir_current_path` does).
// x0 is untouched, so the caller still has the kernel's own answer.
function arm64LinuxEmitStashErrno(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x9, value: 0)
	arm64EmitSubRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x9, lhs: Arm64Register.x9, rhs: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64LinuxEmitErrnoAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x16)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x9, base: Arm64Register.x16, byteOffset: 0)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitStashErrno'

// Publish a KNOWN errno rather than one a syscall just answered — the walk's clear before each step
// (`PosixErrnoCleared`, which is how end-of-iteration reaches `ERROR_NO_MORE_FILES`), and the two places a
// reason has to be invented because no call failed.
function arm64LinuxEmitPublishErrno(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, value ParsedInt)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x9, value: value as MachineWord)
	arm64LinuxEmitErrnoAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x16)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x9, base: Arm64Register.x16, byteOffset: 0)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitPublishErrno'

// ⭐ **THE TAIL OF EVERY SYSCALL IN THIS FILE: a negative answer is a failure, and the reason goes in the
// word.** x0 is left exactly as the kernel wrote it, so a caller that wants a count, a descriptor or a
// sentinel decides that for itself immediately after.
function arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm)
	let succeeded = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: succeeded)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashErrno(asm)

	asm.placeLabel(succeeded)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed'

// The syscall's `0`/`-errno` status becoming the Win32 BOOL the Std tier tests, with the reason stashed on
// the way. NO narrowing: a `svc` answers a full 64-bit word, so a second opinion about its upper half would
// be `emitWidenedCallResult`'s note in reverse.
function arm64LinuxEmitWin32BoolFromSyscall(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm)
	arm64EmitWin32BoolFromZeroStatus(asm)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitWin32BoolFromSyscall'

// Replace a negative x0 with `sentinel`. The Std tier tests for EXACT sentinels — `InvalidHandleValue` is -1
// and `INVALID_FILE_ATTRIBUTES` is 0xFFFFFFFF — and a raw `-errno` is `-2` for a missing file, which would
// read as a live descriptor. `mov` is `movz`/`movk`, neither of which touches the flags, so the compare above
// it still stands at the `csel`.
function arm64LinuxEmitFailureSentinel(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, sentinel MachineWord)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x9, value: sentinel)
	arm64EmitCsel(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x0), ifTrue: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x9), ifFalse: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x0), cond: Arm64CondCode.lt)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitFailureSentinel'

// `AT_FDCWD` into x0 — the last move of every `*at` shuffle, and last precisely because the path it displaces
// arrived there.
function arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, dest Arm64Register)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: dest, value: LinuxAtFdCwd)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd'

// `newfstatat(dirFd, path, statBuf, 0)` with the path already in `pathReg` — the three sites that describe a
// path (the attribute read, the single-`stat` search and the walk's per-entry describe) differ only in which
// register holds the path and which descriptor it is relative to.
function arm64LinuxEmitFstatat(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, dirFd Arm64Register, pathReg Arm64Register, statSlot StackSize)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, src: pathReg)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, slot: statSlot)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: PosixFollowSymlinks as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, src: dirFd)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallNewfstatat)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitFstatat'

// ==========================================================================
// L3 — the two non-scheduler CLOCKS
// ==========================================================================

// `clock_gettime(clockId, &ts)` with the `timespec` in this chunk's own frame — the one call both clock
// chunks open with, and the reason both are chunks at all: the caller's out-param pointer must survive a
// call that writes somewhere ELSE, so a frame has to hold both.
//
// ⚠ **NO STATUS TEST, AND THAT IS NOT THE OMISSION IT LOOKS LIKE.** `clock_gettime` fails only for an
// unknown `clockid_t` (`EINVAL`) or an unreadable buffer (`EFAULT`); the id is a compile-time constant this
// file owns and the buffer is this frame. Neither is reachable, and there is nothing an out-param-shaped op
// with no failure channel could report if they were — `osReadWallClock` returns nothing at all.
// `x0 = sec * 1e9 + nsec` — the two halves of the frame's `timespec` combined into the ONE scalar both
// scheduler-facing clock chunks answer with, which is exactly the `uint64_t` Darwin's `clock_gettime_nsec_np`
// hands over ready-made. Shared by the thread-CPU clock and the monotonic one; the WALL clock does not use it
// because a FILETIME converts each half separately, and its own header says why recombining first would cost
// five centuries of range for an identical answer.
function arm64LinuxEmitTimespecToNanos(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: LinuxTimespecSecSlot)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: PosixNanosPerSecond as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitRuntimeRrr(asm, opcode: Arm64OpMul, dest: Arm64Register.x0, lhs: Arm64Register.x0, rhs: Arm64Register.x1)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: LinuxTimespecNsecSlot)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, lhs: Arm64Register.x0, rhs: Arm64Register.x1)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitTimespecToNanos'

function arm64LinuxReadClockTimespec(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, clockId MachineWord)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: clockId)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: LinuxTimespecSecSlot)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClockGettime)
end 'arm64LinuxReadClockTimespec'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_wall_clock(x0 = ftPtr)
// ==========================================================================

// Write the host's CALENDAR through `ftPtr`, as a Win32 FILETIME — 100-nanosecond ticks since 1601. The
// FILETIME encoding is the OP's contract rather than a Windows detail leaking onto a POSIX lane;
// `Arm64DarwinRuntime.buildArm64DarwinWallClockChunk` carries that argument in full and it is the same one
// here.
//
// ⚠ **WHERE THIS DIVERGES FROM THE DARWIN TWIN IS THE SHAPE OF THE READING, NOT THE ARITHMETIC.**
// `clock_gettime_nsec_np` answers one `uint64_t` of nanoseconds, so that chunk is a single `udiv` plus the
// epoch; `clock_gettime` answers a `timespec`, so the seconds half takes `arm64EmitSecondsToFileTime` — the
// SAME conversion every `struct stat` timestamp on both lanes takes — and only the nanoseconds half divides.
// The two halves are converted where they already are rather than recombined into nanoseconds first: that
// intermediate would buy nothing (`1e9 / 100` IS `1e7`, so the answer is identical) and would overflow a
// 64-bit word some five centuries sooner than the FILETIME it is on its way to.
function buildArm64LinuxWallClockChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotOutPtr = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1 + LinuxTimespecSlots)

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotOutPtr)
	arm64LinuxReadClockTimespec(asm, clockId: LinuxClockRealtime)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: LinuxTimespecSecSlot)
	arm64EmitSecondsToFileTime(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, scratch: Arm64Register.x1)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: LinuxTimespecNsecSlot)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: posixNanosPerFileTimeTick())
	arm64EmitRuntimeRrr(asm, opcode: Arm64OpUdiv, dest: Arm64Register.x1, lhs: Arm64Register.x1, rhs: Arm64Register.x2)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, lhs: Arm64Register.x0, rhs: Arm64Register.x1)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotOutPtr)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: 0)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostWallClockName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxWallClockChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_thread_cpu(x0 = outPtr)
// ==========================================================================

// Write THIS THREAD's consumed CPU time through `outPtr`, in the host's own unit — NANOSECONDS on both POSIX
// lanes, and TSC ticks on Windows, with nothing converting between them. That is `ClockRuntime`'s stated
// contract rather than this chunk's omission: `__thread_cpu_ticks` promises only that two readings may be
// SUBTRACTED, and scaling here to look like Windows would be a guess wearing a unit's name.
//
// ⚠ **`CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID` IS 3 HERE AND 16 ON DARWIN**, which is the one thing that could go wrong
// silently: a wrong id reads a real clock and answers a plausible wrong cost. See `LinuxClockThreadCpuTime`.
//
// The two halves are combined rather than reported separately because the op has ONE out-param: a `timespec`
// becomes `sec * 1e9 + nsec`, which is exactly the `uint64_t` Darwin's call hands over ready-made.
function buildArm64LinuxThreadCpuChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotOutPtr = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1 + LinuxTimespecSlots)

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotOutPtr)
	arm64LinuxReadClockTimespec(asm, clockId: LinuxClockThreadCpuTime)
	arm64LinuxEmitTimespecToNanos(asm)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotOutPtr)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: 0)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostThreadCpuName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxThreadCpuChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_exe_path(x0 = bufPtr, x1 = bufLen) -> x0 = bytes written
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THE RUNNING IMAGE'S PATH, WHICH ON THIS LANE IS NOT A CALL BUT A SYMLINK.** `_NSGetExecutablePath`
// has no Linux counterpart at all; the kernel publishes `/proc/self/exe`, a symbolic link whose TARGET is
// the path, so this chunk is one `readlinkat(AT_FDCWD, "/proc/self/exe", buf, bufLen)`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **`readlinkat` DOES NOT NUL-TERMINATE AND DOES NOT REPORT TRUNCATION**, and those are the two facts
// this chunk exists for. It answers the byte count it wrote, writes no terminator, and — when the link does
// not fit — SILENTLY TRUNCATES and answers exactly `bufLen`. Both are handled here in the vocabulary
// `osExecutablePath` already speaks (`ManagedDirectoryRuntime.emitFilledBufferVerdict`):
//
//   • `n < 0` ⇒ answer **0**, which the Std verdict reads as "refused" and `__proc_exe_path` turns into
//     `pathUnavailable`. ⚠ No errno is stashed, deliberately: nothing consumes an `osLastError` after this
//     op, and stashing would put the `.data` word `mrt_host_last_error` reads behind a facility bit that
//     does not lay it out (`arm64LinuxRuntimeGlobals` gates it on the FILESYSTEM families).
//   • `n >= bufLen` ⇒ answer **n unchanged**, which is NOT `< capacity`, so the Std loop doubles its buffer
//     and asks again. That is precisely why the terminator is written only on the fitting path: a `buf[n]`
//     store at `n == bufLen` is a one-byte overrun of the caller's buffer, and the caller is about to throw
//     that buffer away regardless.
//   • otherwise ⇒ terminate at `buf[n]` and answer `n`.
//
// ⚠ **THE ARGUMENTS SHUFFLE AND THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING** for `buildArm64LinuxFileOpenChunk`'s reason: the
// path constant materialises in x0 (the shared `.rdata` blob channel hands it there), so it moves to x1
// BEFORE `AT_FDCWD` takes the x0 it vacated.
function buildArm64LinuxExePathChunk(globalData GlobalDataTable) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotBufPtr = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotBufLen = 1 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(2)

	let unavailable = asm.newLabel()
	let finish = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotBufPtr)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotBufLen)

	arm64EmitCStringArg(asm, globalData: globalData, bytes: LinuxProcSelfExe)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, src: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotBufPtr)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotBufLen)
	arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallReadlinkat)

	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lt, label: unavailable)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotBufLen)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64Register.x0, rhs: Arm64Register.x1)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.hs, label: finish)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotBufPtr)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, lhs: Arm64Register.x1, rhs: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: NulByte as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitStoreByte(asm, src: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x1)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: finish)

	asm.placeLabel(unavailable)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: 0)

	asm.placeLabel(finish)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostExePathName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxExePathChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_cpu_count() -> x0 = usable processors | -1
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`sched_getaffinity` AND A POPCOUNT, BECAUSE `sysconf` IS A LIBC FUNCTION AND THIS IMAGE LINKS NO
// LIBC.** Two routes were available and this is the one taken:
//
//   • `sched_getaffinity(0, sizeof(cpu_set_t), &mask)` — one syscall, no filesystem, and it reports the CPUs
//     THIS PROCESS MAY ACTUALLY USE. That is what a parallelism hint wants, and it is what `nproc` itself
//     answers (MEASURED on a guest whose affinity mask held 7 of 8 CPUs: this answers 7, and so does
//     `nproc`, while a machine-wide count answers 8).
//   • Reading and parsing `/sys/devices/system/cpu/online` — a decimal-range parser, and a dependency on a
//     MOUNTED sysfs from a chunk whose whole family is supposed to touch no filesystem. `managedFile` is a
//     separate facility on this lane precisely so a machine query does not drag one in.
//
// ⚠ **THE COUNT IS A POPCOUNT, AND AArch64 HAS NO SCALAR POPCOUNT INSTRUCTION** — `CNT` is a NEON op over a
// vector register. So the bits are counted with Kernighan's `x &= x - 1`, whose iteration count is the
// number of SET bits rather than 64 per word: on a real machine that is the processor count itself.
//
// ⚠ **THE ANSWER IS NOT CLAMPED HERE.** A failed syscall reports `LinuxCpuCountUnavailable` (-1), which is
// `sysconf`'s own failure spelling and is what `CpuParallelRuntime.buildCpuCount`'s signed `< 1 ⇒ 1` clamp
// already reads — see `lowerOsCpuCount` for why the clamp must stay in exactly one place.
function buildArm64LinuxCpuCountChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotMask = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(runtimeFrameSlots(LinuxCpuSetBytes))

	let count = asm.newLabel()
	let wordStep = asm.newLabel()
	let bitStep = asm.newLabel()
	let nextWord = asm.newLabel()
	let finish = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitZeroSlots(asm, firstSlot: SlotMask, slotCount: runtimeFrameSlots(LinuxCpuSetBytes))

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: LinuxAffinityWhoIsSelf)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxCpuSetBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotMask)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallSchedGetaffinity)

	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: count)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: LinuxCpuCountUnavailable)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: finish)

	// x0 = the running count, x1 = the cursor, x2 = words left, x3 = the word being drained, x4 = its scratch.
	asm.placeLabel(count)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: 0)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotMask)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: runtimeFrameSlots(LinuxCpuSetBytes))

	asm.placeLabel(wordStep)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, label: finish)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: 0)

	asm.placeLabel(bitStep)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, label: nextWord)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, base: Arm64Register.x3, imm: -1)
	arm64EmitRuntimeRrr(asm, opcode: Arm64OpAnd, dest: Arm64Register.x3, lhs: Arm64Register.x3, rhs: Arm64Register.x4)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: bitStep)

	asm.placeLabel(nextWord)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x1, imm: SpillSlotBytes as Imm32)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x2, imm: -1)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: wordStep)

	asm.placeLabel(finish)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostCpuCountName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxCpuCountChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_last_error() -> x0 = the Win32 code the Std tier classifies
// ==========================================================================

// `GetLastError()` on a lane with no `errno`: read the word every chunk here stashes into, and hand it to the
// shared translator. The MAPPING is `Arm64PosixRuntime.arm64EmitErrnoToWin32`'s and has no lane in it.
function buildArm64LinuxLastErrorChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	arm64LinuxEmitErrnoAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x16)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x16, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitErrnoToWin32(asm)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostLastErrorName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxLastErrorChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_file_open(x0 = path, x1 = flags, x2 = mode) -> x0 = fd | -1
// ==========================================================================

// `openat(AT_FDCWD, path, flags, mode)`. It is a CHUNK rather than an inline isel sequence for the two
// reasons the isel's `lowerOsFileOpenLinux` names: the kernel answers `-errno` where the Std tier tests for
// exactly `-1`, and the reason has to be stashed for the `osLastError` that follows.
//
// ⚠ **THE ARGUMENTS SHUFFLE UP ONE AND THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING**: each move must run before the register it
// reads is overwritten, so they go from the top down and `AT_FDCWD` lands last, in the x0 the path has just
// vacated.
function buildArm64LinuxFileOpenChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, src: Arm64Register.x2)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, src: Arm64Register.x1)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, src: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallOpenat)

	arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm)
	arm64LinuxEmitFailureSentinel(asm, sentinel: PosixInvalidHandle)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostFileOpenName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxFileOpenChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_read_file / mrt_host_write_file
//   (x0 = fd, x1 = buf, x2 = len, x3 = bytesOutPtr) -> x0 = BOOL
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`ReadFile`/`WriteFile`'s SHAPE OVER A CALL THAT ANSWERS THE COUNT IN x0.** The Win32 pair return a
// BOOL and write the transferred count through a pointer; `read`/`write` return the count (or `-errno`) and
// write nothing. So the pointer has to OUTLIVE the syscall, which is this file's structural reason for a
// chunk, and the two answers have to be separated afterwards.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A FAILURE STORES `0` THROUGH THE OUT-PARAM AND NEVER THE NEGATIVE.** `ConsoleRuntime`'s
// `__con_read_stdin` publishes that word directly as an `Array`'s LENGTH, and a negative length is a corrupt
// array rather than a failed read — the caller sees a record claiming 2^64-1 bytes.
//
// ⚠ **ONE BUILDER, TWO CHUNKS**: read and write differ in a syscall number and in nothing else — not in the
// argument order, not in the verdict, not in the out-param — so writing them twice would be one transfer
// sequence with two chances to disagree about which register holds the length. (`Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s
// `buildArm64DarwinTransferChunk` is the same argument on the other lane.)
function buildArm64LinuxTransferChunk(name ByteArray, syscallNumber MachineWord) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotOutPtr = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)

	let transferred = asm.newLabel()
	let answered = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotOutPtr)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: syscallNumber)

	arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotOutPtr)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: transferred)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: 0)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: Win32False as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	asm.placeLabel(transferred)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: Win32True as MachineWord)

	asm.placeLabel(answered)
	return asm.finishFunction(name)
end 'buildArm64LinuxTransferChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_file_size(x0 = fd, x1 = sizeOutPtr) -> x0 = BOOL
// ==========================================================================

// `GetFileSizeEx` — a BOOL plus a 64-bit size through a pointer, which is `fstat` plus one field read.
//
// ⚠ **NOTHING IS WRITTEN THROUGH THE POINTER ON FAILURE, AND THE CALLER ALREADY RELIES ON THAT.** The scratch
// word comes from `ManagedFileRuntime.emitWin32OutWord`, i.e. from `__mm_alloc`, which hands out ZEROED
// memory as a stated guarantee — and `emitWin32BoolOutcome` reads the word on both paths.
function buildArm64LinuxFileSizeChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotOutPtr = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotStat = 1 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1 + runtimeFrameSlots(arm64LinuxStatLayout().bytes))

	let measured = asm.newLabel()
	let answered = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotOutPtr)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotStat)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallFstat)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: measured)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: Win32False as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	asm.placeLabel(measured)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotStat)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: Arm64LinuxStatSizeOffset)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotOutPtr)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: Win32True as MachineWord)

	asm.placeLabel(answered)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostFileSizeName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxFileSizeChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_file_delete(x0 = pathPtr) -> x0 = BOOL
// mrt_host_dir_create(x0 = pathPtr) -> x0 = BOOL
// ==========================================================================

// `DeleteFileA` ⇒ `unlinkat(AT_FDCWD, path, 0)` and `CreateDirectoryA(path, NULL)` ⇒
// `mkdirat(AT_FDCWD, path, 0777)`. ONE BUILDER: both take a path and a third word — a flag word that must be
// zero for the unlink, a permission mask for the mkdir — and answer a verdict the same way, so the only
// honest difference is a number and a syscall.
//
// The Win32 directory create carries no permissions at all (a new directory inherits its parent's ACL), so
// the POSIX side hands the widest mask over and lets the process umask narrow it, which is the closest thing
// to "inherit" POSIX has (`PosixDirectoryMode`).
function buildArm64LinuxPathOpChunk(name ByteArray, syscallNumber MachineWord, thirdArgument MachineWord) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, src: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: thirdArgument)
	arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: syscallNumber)
	arm64LinuxEmitWin32BoolFromSyscall(asm)

	return asm.finishFunction(name)
end 'buildArm64LinuxPathOpChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_file_rename(x0 = oldPathPtr, x1 = newPathPtr) -> x0 = BOOL
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`MoveFileExA(old, new, MOVEFILE_REPLACE_EXISTING | MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH)` IS TWO PROMISES AND
// `renameat` KEEPS ONLY ONE OF THEM.** The REPLACE is free: POSIX `rename` is defined to replace an existing
// destination atomically. The WRITE-THROUGH is not: Win32 promises the operation has reached the disk before
// it returns, and POSIX records a rename's durability in the DESTINATION DIRECTORY's metadata — so keeping
// the second promise means opening that directory and `fsync`ing it. (`Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s twin carries the
// same two paragraphs; what differs here is that the prefix goes in a frame buffer rather than a `malloc`.)
//
// ⚠ **THE DURABILITY STEP IS BEST-EFFORT AND ITS FAILURES ARE DELIBERATELY NOT REPORTED.** The rename has
// already happened and is already visible; a caller told "the rename failed" because the parent directory
// could not be opened would delete or retry something that is in fact in place. So every exit below the
// rename answers TRUE, and the only thing that can answer FALSE is the rename itself.
//
// ⚠ **AND NOTHING BELOW THE RENAME MAY STASH AN errno**, for the same reason: the word is read by an
// `osLastError` the Std tier emits immediately after this call, and a `fsync` that failed would be blamed for
// a rename that succeeded. The three syscalls in the durability step therefore go through `arm64EmitSyscall`
// directly rather than through `arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed`.
function buildArm64LinuxFileRenameChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotNewPath = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotDirFd = 1 as StackSize
	let SlotPrefix = 2 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(2 + runtimeFrameSlots(MaxPathBytes))

	let renamed = asm.newLabel()
	let released = asm.newLabel()
	let answered = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotNewPath)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, src: Arm64Register.x1)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, src: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallRenameat)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: renamed)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: Win32False as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	asm.placeLabel(renamed)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotNewPath)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotPrefix)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: LinuxDirPrefixName)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: released)

	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotPrefix)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: LinuxOpenReadOnlyFlags)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: PosixNoFileMode as MachineWord)
	arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallOpenat)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotDirFd)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lt, label: released)

	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallFsync)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotDirFd)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClose)

	asm.placeLabel(released)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: Win32True as MachineWord)

	asm.placeLabel(answered)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostFileRenameName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxFileRenameChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_file_attributes(x0 = pathPtr) -> x0 = dwFileAttributes
// ==========================================================================

// `GetFileAttributesA` — the mask, or `INVALID_FILE_ATTRIBUTES` for a path that names nothing. It is a `stat`
// and the shared mask derivation, which is what keeps `__md_exists`'s answer and the DIRECTORY bit `__mf_stat`
// publishes derived from ONE reading of `st_mode`.
function buildArm64LinuxFileAttributesChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotStat = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(runtimeFrameSlots(arm64LinuxStatLayout().bytes))

	let described = asm.newLabel()
	let answered = asm.newLabel()

	arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x9)
	arm64LinuxEmitFstatat(asm, dirFd: Arm64Register.x9, pathReg: Arm64Register.x0, statSlot: SlotStat)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: described)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: InvalidFileAttributes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	asm.placeLabel(described)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStat)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostAttrsFromModeName)

	asm.placeLabel(answered)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostFileAttributesName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxFileAttributesChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_dir_current_path(x0 = bufPtr, x1 = bufLen) -> x0 = bytes written
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`GetCurrentDirectoryA`'s THREE-WAY ANSWER, AND THE RAW SYSCALL IS THE EASIER HALF OF IT.** The op's
// contract (`ManagedDirectoryRuntime.emitFilledBufferVerdict`) reads `0` as failure, `< capacity` as "it fits,
// NUL included" and anything else as "too small". Linux's `getcwd` SYSCALL — unlike the libc wrapper Darwin's
// lane calls, which answers a pointer or NULL — answers the byte LENGTH INCLUDING the terminator, or
// `-errno`. So the fit case is one subtraction and the classification is one compare against `ERANGE`.
//
// ⚠ **THE SUBTRACTION IS NOT COSMETIC.** Answering the kernel's count would report `capacity` for a path that
// exactly fills the buffer — which the contract reads as "too small" — and would send `__md_current_path`
// round its doubling loop for a path that had in fact fitted.
//
// ⛔ **REPORTING "TOO SMALL" FOR A REAL FAILURE WOULD SPIN THE CALLER'S DOUBLING LOOP FOREVER**, and reporting
// a failure for a too-small buffer would throw on a long path. That is why this reads the errno rather than
// answering one thing for both.
function buildArm64LinuxDirCurrentPathChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotBufLen = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)

	let refused = asm.newLabel()
	let tooSmall = asm.newLabel()
	let answered = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotBufLen)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallGetcwd)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lt, label: refused)

	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x0, imm: -1)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	// `arm64LinuxEmitStashErrno` leaves the POSITIVE errno in x9, which is exactly the value the `ERANGE`
	// test needs — so the reason is published and classified from one negation.
	asm.placeLabel(refused)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashErrno(asm)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x9, imm: PosixErrnoResultTooLarge as Imm32)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.eq, label: tooSmall)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: 0)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	// The "too small" answer is the CAPACITY ITSELF and not a required size, because POSIX has no call that
	// reports the length of a working directory — `__md_current_path` therefore DOUBLES rather than retrying
	// at a named size.
	asm.placeLabel(tooSmall)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotBufLen)

	asm.placeLabel(answered)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostDirCurrentPathName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxDirCurrentPathChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_linux_glob_match(x0 = pattern, x1 = name) -> x0 = 1 | 0
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`fnmatch(pattern, string, 0)`, WRITTEN OUT, BECAUSE A STATIC IMAGE LINKS NO libc.** The Darwin lane
// calls the real thing; this is the classic backtracking matcher over `*` and `?` — the only two wildcards
// `FindFirstFileA` honours and the only two the corpus writes.
//
// ⚠ **IT REPRODUCES `fnmatch`'s FLAG-ZERO BEHAVIOUR, NOT `FNM_PATHNAME`'s.** A `*` matches a leading dot (so
// `*` yields `.` and `..`, exactly as `FindFirstFileA` does, and the Std tier's dot filter removes them) and
// nothing here treats `/` specially — which costs nothing, because the pattern reaching it is always a BASE
// NAME (`mrt_host_base_name` split it).
//
// ⚠ **Win32 WILDCARD SEMANTICS AND `fnmatch`'s ARE NOT THE SAME, AND THE CORPUS DOES NOT REACH THE
// DIFFERENCE** — the same paragraph the Darwin lane carries, and the same conclusion: they agree on `*` and
// `?` over ordinary names, and a case that reaches Win32's `*.*` or its trailing-dot rules is a design ruling
// rather than something either lowering may settle.
//
// ⭐ **THE BACKTRACK IS A SAVED PAIR, NOT RECURSION.** `star` remembers the pattern position just past the
// last `*` and `resume` the name position it was first tried at; a mismatch rewinds to those and advances
// `resume` by one, which is what lets one `*` absorb any number of characters without a stack.
function buildArm64LinuxGlobMatchChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	let pattern = Arm64Register.x0
	let name = Arm64Register.x1
	let star = Arm64Register.x2
	let resume = Arm64Register.x3
	let patternByte = Arm64Register.x4
	let nameByte = Arm64Register.x5

	let step = asm.newLabel()
	let notQuestion = asm.newLabel()
	let notStar = asm.newLabel()
	let advance = asm.newLabel()
	let backtrack = asm.newLabel()
	let nameSpent = asm.newLabel()
	let trailingStars = asm.newLabel()
	let matched = asm.newLabel()
	let refused = asm.newLabel()
	let answered = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: star, value: 0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: resume, value: 0)

	asm.placeLabel(step)
	arm64EmitLoadByte(asm, dest: nameByte, base: name)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: nameByte, label: nameSpent)
	arm64EmitLoadByte(asm, dest: patternByte, base: pattern)

	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: patternByte, imm: PosixGlobQuestionByte as Imm32)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne, label: notQuestion)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: advance)

	asm.placeLabel(notQuestion)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: patternByte, imm: PosixGlobStarByte as Imm32)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne, label: notStar)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: pattern, base: pattern, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: star, src: pattern)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: resume, src: name)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: step)

	// A literal byte. It cannot be the pattern's NUL here — the name still has a byte, and a NUL never equals
	// it — so the equal case really is a match and the unequal case really is a mismatch.
	asm.placeLabel(notStar)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: patternByte, rhs: nameByte)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne, label: backtrack)

	asm.placeLabel(advance)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: pattern, base: pattern, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: name, base: name, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: step)

	asm.placeLabel(backtrack)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: star, label: refused)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: pattern, src: star)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: resume, base: resume, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: name, src: resume)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: step)

	// The name is spent: what is left of the pattern must be `*`s and nothing else.
	asm.placeLabel(nameSpent)
	asm.placeLabel(trailingStars)
	arm64EmitLoadByte(asm, dest: patternByte, base: pattern)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: patternByte, label: matched)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: patternByte, imm: PosixGlobStarByte as Imm32)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne, label: refused)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: pattern, base: pattern, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: trailingStars)

	asm.placeLabel(matched)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: 1)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	asm.placeLabel(refused)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: 0)

	asm.placeLabel(answered)
	return asm.finishFunction(LinuxGlobMatchName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxGlobMatchChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_linux_dir_prefix(x0 = path, x1 = destBuf) -> x0 = 1 | 0
// ==========================================================================

// The DIRECTORY half of a path, written into a caller-supplied `MAX_PATH` buffer. Three shapes, and each is a
// real one the corpus writes:
//
//   `"temp/x/*.txt"` ⇒ `"temp/x"`   — the prefix without its trailing separator
//   `"/x"`           ⇒ `"/"`        — the root, which is the ONE case where the separator IS the directory
//   `"*.txt"`        ⇒ `"."`        — no separator at all means the CURRENT directory
//
// ⭐ **IT WRITES INTO THE CALLER'S BUFFER WHERE DARWIN'S `malloc`s ONE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE.**
// A static image has no allocator to call from a chunk; both callers here have a frame or a mapped page to
// lend instead.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A PREFIX THAT DOES NOT FIT IS A REFUSAL, NEVER A TRUNCATION.** A truncated directory path is not a
// path that fails to open — it can NAME A DIFFERENT DIRECTORY THAT EXISTS, and the walk would then list the
// wrong one and report success. So the length is checked before the copy and a `0` answer means "did not
// fit", which `mrt_host_dir_find_first` publishes as `ENAMETOOLONG`.
function buildArm64LinuxDirPrefixChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotPath = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotDest = 1 as StackSize
	let SlotLength = 2 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(3)

	let currentDirectory = asm.newLabel()
	let tooLong = asm.newLabel()
	let answered = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotPath)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotDest)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostBaseNameName)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotPath)
	arm64EmitSubRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, lhs: Arm64Register.x0, rhs: Arm64Register.x1)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: currentDirectory)

	// The prefix INCLUDES the separator, so the directory is one byte shorter — except at the root, where
	// dropping it would leave the empty string and an `openat("")` fails with ENOENT.
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: 1)
	arm64EmitSubRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, lhs: Arm64Register.x0, rhs: Arm64Register.x1)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCsel(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x2), ifTrue: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x1), ifFalse: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x2), cond: Arm64CondCode.eq)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, imm: PosixPathStringCapacity as Imm32)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.gt, label: tooLong)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotLength)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotPath)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotDest)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotLength)
	arm64EmitCopyCStringBounded(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, dest: Arm64Register.x2, limit: Arm64Register.x3, scratch: Arm64Register.x4)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: 1)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	// No separator: the two bytes `"."` — written directly rather than copied out of a constant, because a
	// two-byte string is shorter than the code that would address one.
	asm.placeLabel(currentDirectory)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotDest)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: DotByte as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitStoreByte(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x2)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x2, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: NulByte as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitStoreByte(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x2)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: 1)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	asm.placeLabel(tooLong)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: 0)

	asm.placeLabel(answered)
	return asm.finishFunction(LinuxDirPrefixName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxDirPrefixChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_dir_find_next(x0 = search, x1 = findDataPtr) -> x0 = BOOL
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **`FindNextFileA` — AND ALSO THE WHOLE OF `FindFirstFileA`'s FIRST ENTRY**, which is why `find_first`
// below calls this rather than carrying a second copy of the walk. Win32's first and subsequent entries come
// from one enumeration; so do these.
//
// The loop: clear the errno word, take the next directory entry, match it against the pattern, and describe
// the first one that matches. An exhausted enumeration leaves the word CLEAR, which the caller reads as
// `ERROR_NO_MORE_FILES` (`PosixErrnoCleared`), and a `getdents64` that FAILS stashes its own reason — so the
// same FALSE carries both answers exactly as Win32's does.
//
// ⚠ **`getdents64` REFILLS A BUFFER, WHERE `readdir` ANSWERS ONE ENTRY.** That is the one structural
// difference from the Darwin walk: the search carries a cursor and a filled count, and the syscall runs only
// when the cursor has caught up. A `0` from it is end-of-directory; a negative is a failure.
//
// ⚠ **A SEARCH WITH A NEGATIVE `dirfd` IS EXHAUSTED, NOT BROKEN** — the single-`stat` route already reported
// its one entry. The guard is INSIDE the loop head, after the errno clear, so that exit reports
// end-of-iteration rather than a stale failure.
//
// ⚠ **`fstatat`'s VERDICT IS DELIBERATELY DISCARDED AND THE BUFFER IS ZEROED FIRST.** A dangling symbolic link
// is a real directory entry that Win32 reports; skipping it would silently shorten a listing, and reading a
// stat buffer the call did not fill would report the PREVIOUS entry's size and timestamps for it.
function buildArm64LinuxDirFindNextChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotSearch = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotFindData = 1 as StackSize
	let SlotName = 2 as StackSize
	let SlotStat = 3 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(3 + runtimeFrameSlots(arm64LinuxStatLayout().bytes))

	let nextEntry = asm.newLabel()
	let haveEntry = asm.newLabel()
	let exhausted = asm.newLabel()
	let answered = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotFindData)

	asm.placeLabel(nextEntry)
	arm64LinuxEmitPublishErrno(asm, value: PosixErrnoCleared)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x10, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x11, base: Arm64Register.x10, byteOffset: LinuxSearchDirFdOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x11, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lt, label: exhausted)

	// Is there an entry left in the buffer? Cursor and filled are both byte counts into the entries region.
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x12, base: Arm64Register.x10, byteOffset: LinuxSearchCursorOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x13, base: Arm64Register.x10, byteOffset: LinuxSearchFilledOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64Register.x12, rhs: Arm64Register.x13)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lt, label: haveEntry)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, src: Arm64Register.x11)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x10, imm: LinuxSearchEntriesOffset)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: LinuxSearchEntriesBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallGetdents64)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.le, label: exhausted)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x10, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x10, byteOffset: LinuxSearchFilledOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x12, value: 0)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x12, base: Arm64Register.x10, byteOffset: LinuxSearchCursorOffset as MachineWord)

	// `entry = search + entriesOffset + cursor`, and the cursor steps by the entry's own `d_reclen`.
	asm.placeLabel(haveEntry)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x10, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x12, base: Arm64Register.x10, byteOffset: LinuxSearchCursorOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x14, base: Arm64Register.x10, imm: LinuxSearchEntriesOffset)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x14, lhs: Arm64Register.x14, rhs: Arm64Register.x12)
	arm64EmitLoadWord32At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x13, base: Arm64Register.x14, byteOffset: LinuxDirentRecordLengthOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x15, value: LinuxDirentRecordLengthMask as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitRuntimeRrr(asm, opcode: Arm64OpAnd, dest: Arm64Register.x13, lhs: Arm64Register.x13, rhs: Arm64Register.x15)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x12, lhs: Arm64Register.x12, rhs: Arm64Register.x13)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x12, base: Arm64Register.x10, byteOffset: LinuxSearchCursorOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x14, base: Arm64Register.x14, imm: LinuxDirentNameOffset)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x14, slot: SlotName)

	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x10, imm: LinuxSearchPatternOffset)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, src: Arm64Register.x14)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: LinuxGlobMatchName)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: nextEntry)

	// Describe it, RELATIVE to the open directory — which is why this walk never builds a path and never owns
	// a path buffer.
	arm64EmitZeroSlots(asm, firstSlot: SlotStat, slotCount: runtimeFrameSlots(arm64LinuxStatLayout().bytes))
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x10, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x11, base: Arm64Register.x10, byteOffset: LinuxSearchDirFdOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x14, slot: SlotName)
	arm64LinuxEmitFstatat(asm, dirFd: Arm64Register.x11, pathReg: Arm64Register.x14, statSlot: SlotStat)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotFindData)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotStat)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotName)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostFillFindDataName)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: Win32True as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	asm.placeLabel(exhausted)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: Win32False as MachineWord)

	asm.placeLabel(answered)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostDirFindNextName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxDirFindNextChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_dir_find_first(x0 = patternPtr, x1 = findDataPtr) -> x0 = search | -1
// ==========================================================================

// `FindFirstFileA` — open the enumeration a glob names and report its first entry, or answer
// `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE` with the errno word telling the caller why.
//
// ⭐ **TWO ROUTES, AND WHICH ONE IS TAKEN IS A PROPERTY OF THE PATTERN**: a base name with no `*` or `?` names
// one path and is answered by one `stat`; anything else opens the directory and walks it. See
// `mrt_host_has_wildcard` for why the fast path is more faithful and not merely faster.
//
// ⚠ **AN EMPTY MATCH IS `ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND`, WHICH IS Win32's ANSWER AND NOT THIS LANE'S INVENTION.**
// `FindFirstFileA` on a directory that exists but holds nothing matching fails with exactly that. A walk that
// ended because `getdents64` FAILED keeps its own errno instead, so a real IO error is not reported as a
// missing file.
//
// ⚠ **THE TEARDOWN ON THAT PATH NEEDS NO SAVE-AND-REPUBLISH DANCE, WHERE DARWIN'S DOES.** `mrt_host_dir_find_close`
// here is a `close` and a `munmap`, and NEITHER stashes: only a failure that a caller will be told about goes
// in the word. Darwin's teardown runs `free`, which is allocator code that can move `errno`, which is why
// that lane captures the reason first.
function buildArm64LinuxDirFindFirstChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotPattern = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotFindData = 1 as StackSize
	let SlotGlob = 2 as StackSize
	let SlotSearch = 3 as StackSize
	let SlotStat = 4 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(4 + runtimeFrameSlots(arm64LinuxStatLayout().bytes))

	let walk = asm.newLabel()
	let matched = asm.newLabel()
	let nameTooLong = asm.newLabel()
	let releaseAndRefuse = asm.newLabel()
	let publishReason = asm.newLabel()
	let refused = asm.newLabel()
	let answered = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotPattern)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotFindData)

	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostBaseNameName)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotGlob)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostHasWildcardName)
	arm64EmitCbnzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: walk)

	// One path: `stat` it, and hand back a search that is already exhausted.
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x14, slot: SlotPattern)
	arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x9)
	arm64LinuxEmitFstatat(asm, dirFd: Arm64Register.x9, pathReg: Arm64Register.x14, statSlot: SlotStat)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lt, label: refused)
	arm64LinuxEmitAllocateSearch(asm, searchSlot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: refused)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotFindData)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotStat)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotGlob)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostFillFindDataName)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	// A glob: the page comes FIRST, because it is also where the directory prefix is built — this lane has no
	// `malloc` to borrow a scratch buffer from.
	asm.placeLabel(walk)
	arm64LinuxEmitAllocateSearch(asm, searchSlot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: refused)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotPattern)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x10, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x10, imm: LinuxSearchPrefixOffset)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: LinuxDirPrefixName)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: nameTooLong)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x10, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x10, imm: LinuxSearchPrefixOffset)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: Arm64LinuxOpenDirectoryFlags)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: PosixNoFileMode as MachineWord)
	arm64LinuxEmitAtFdCwd(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallOpenat)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lt, label: releaseAndRefuse)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x10, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x10, byteOffset: LinuxSearchDirFdOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotGlob)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x10, imm: LinuxSearchPatternOffset)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: PosixPathStringCapacity as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitCopyCStringBounded(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, dest: Arm64Register.x2, limit: Arm64Register.x3, scratch: Arm64Register.x4)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotFindData)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostDirFindNextName)
	arm64EmitCbnzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: matched)

	// Nothing matched. An exhausted walk left the word CLEAR; a failed one left its own reason there.
	arm64LinuxEmitErrnoAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x16)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x9, base: Arm64Register.x16, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitCbnzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x9, label: publishReason)
	arm64LinuxEmitPublishErrno(asm, value: PosixErrnoNoSuchFileOrDirectory)

	asm.placeLabel(publishReason)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: releaseAndRefuse)

	asm.placeLabel(nameTooLong)
	arm64LinuxEmitPublishErrno(asm, value: LinuxErrnoNameTooLong)

	asm.placeLabel(releaseAndRefuse)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostDirFindCloseName)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: refused)

	asm.placeLabel(matched)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: answered)

	asm.placeLabel(refused)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: PosixInvalidHandle)

	asm.placeLabel(answered)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostDirFindFirstName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxDirFindFirstChunk'

// Map one page for a search, leaving it in x0 AND in `searchSlot`, or 0 in both. `MAP_ANON` zeroes it, so the
// cursor, the filled count and the pattern's first byte are already right; only the descriptor is written,
// and it is written to the "no directory" sentinel so that a caller which never opens one still hands back an
// object `find_next` reads correctly.
//
// ⚠ **A REFUSED `mmap` ANSWERS A SMALL NEGATIVE, NOT NULL**, so the test is a sign test and the answer is
// normalised to 0 for the `cbz` every caller writes. The reason is stashed, because a caller is about to be
// told this failed.
function arm64LinuxEmitAllocateSearch(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, searchSlot StackSize)
	let mapped = asm.newLabel()
	let recorded = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: MmapKernelChoosesBase)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxSearchBytes)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: MmapProtReadWrite)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: MmapAnonPrivateLinux)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, value: MmapAnonFd)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x5, value: MmapNoOffset)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallMmap)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashIfFailed(asm)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.gt, label: mapped)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: 0)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: searchSlot)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: recorded)

	asm.placeLabel(mapped)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: searchSlot)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x9, value: LinuxNoDirectory)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x9, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxSearchDirFdOffset as MachineWord)

	asm.placeLabel(recorded)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitAllocateSearch'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_dir_find_close(x0 = search)
// ==========================================================================

// `FindClose` — release the enumeration and everything it owns, which on this lane is one descriptor and one
// mapping. Idempotence is the Std tier's (`__md_close` stamps its closed sentinel and never calls twice);
// what is here is that a search opened by the single-`stat` route has NO directory, so the `close` is guarded
// while the `munmap` is not.
//
// ⚠ **NEITHER SYSCALL STASHES.** `find_first`'s empty-match path reads the errno word AFTER calling this, and
// a `close` that failed would overwrite the reason it is about to publish.
function buildArm64LinuxDirFindCloseChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotSearch = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)

	let unmap = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxSearchDirFdOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lt, label: unmap)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClose)

	asm.placeLabel(unmap)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSearch)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxSearchBytes)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallMunmap)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostDirFindCloseName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxDirFindCloseChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// L4 — THE GREEN-THREAD FLOOR'S HOST OBJECTS
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHAT THIS LANE HAS THAT DARWIN'S DOES NOT: ONE PRIMITIVE INSTEAD OF THREE.** The Darwin surface
// builds an event out of a mutex, a condition variable and a signal word, because a condvar's wait is only
// atomic with respect to a mutex it is handed. `futex` needs neither: `FUTEX_WAIT(uaddr, val)` compares and
// sleeps INSIDE the kernel, so the compare cannot be lost between the test and the sleep. That single fact
// removes the mutex, the condvar, `pthread_mutexattr_t`, four `_init`/`_destroy` calls and the 112 bytes of
// opaque struct from every object below.
//
// ⚠ **AND ONE THING IT HAS THAT DARWIN DOES NOT NEED: A WRITABLE THREAD POINTER.** `pthread_getspecific` is
// a base-plus-index load off a TSD array libSystem maintains; here there is nothing to maintain one, so
// `mrt_host_tls_alloc` maps a page and publishes it in `TPIDR_EL0` — a register nothing else on a static
// image touches. `Arm64Backend.arm64EmitThreadPointer` carries the two-register split.

// `futex(uaddr, op, val, timeout, uaddr2, val3)` — the one call shape all four users below marshal, with the
// address taken from a frame slot because every caller has just clobbered the register it arrived in.
//
// ⚠ **THE ANSWER IS DELIBERATELY NOT TESTED.** A `FUTEX_WAIT` returns for four reasons — woken, `EAGAIN`
// (the word had already changed), `EINTR` (a signal) and `ETIMEDOUT` — and the correct response to ALL of
// them is the same: re-check the predicate. Every caller here does, in a loop, which is what makes a
// spurious return harmless rather than a lost wakeup. A `FUTEX_WAKE` answers how many threads it woke, and
// nothing here asks.
function arm64LinuxEmitFutex(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, addrSlot StackSize, operation MachineWord, value MachineWord, timeoutSlot StackSize, hasTimeout bool)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: addrSlot)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: operation)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: value)
	arm64LinuxEmitFutexTimeout(asm, timeoutSlot: timeoutSlot, hasTimeout: hasTimeout)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, value: LinuxFutexUnusedArg)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x5, value: LinuxFutexUnusedArg)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallFutex)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitFutex'

// x3 = `&timeout` or NULL — the one argument of the six above that is not a plain immediate.
function arm64LinuxEmitFutexTimeout(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, timeoutSlot StackSize, hasTimeout bool)
	if hasTimeout 'timed'
		arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, slot: timeoutSlot)
	end 'timed' else 'untimed'
		arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: LinuxFutexNoTimeout)
	end 'untimed'
end 'arm64LinuxEmitFutexTimeout'

// `dest = CAS(*addrReg, expected, replacement)` as a BOOLEAN — 1 if the store landed. The runtime-asm
// spelling of the encoder's LL/SC pair, so a chunk here reads in `Arm64Register`s like everything else
// around it.
//
// ⚠ **IT CLOBBERS x16 AND x17**, which are this file's address scratch and the encoder's LL/SC status
// register. No caller below holds anything in either across one.
// `dest = CAS(*[addrSlot], expected, replacement)` with both operands as literals — the three call sites
// below differ only in those two numbers.
function arm64LinuxEmitCasImm(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, dest Arm64Register, addrSlot StackSize, expected MachineWord, replacement MachineWord)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: addrSlot)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: expected)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: replacement)
	arm64EmitAtomicCas(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(dest), addrReg: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x0), expectedReg: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x1), replacementReg: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x2))
end 'arm64LinuxEmitCasImm'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_monotonic_clock(x0 = ticksPtr, x1 = freqPtr)
// ==========================================================================

// The MONOTONIC counter and its frequency — Windows's QueryPerformanceCounter/QueryPerformanceFrequency, and
// the reading `__gt_now_ns` computes every deadline in this scheduler from.
//
// The frequency is a CONSTANT 1e9 because the reading is already nanoseconds, which makes `__gt_now_ns`'s
// ticks→nanoseconds scaling the IDENTITY on this lane exactly as it is on the other one — the strongest form
// that split can take, with no rounding of its own.
//
// ⚠ **THE SHAPE DIFFERS FROM THE DARWIN TWIN AND THE CLOCK ID DIFFERS FROM IT TOO.** `clock_gettime` answers
// a `timespec` where `clock_gettime_nsec_np` answers one scalar, so the two halves are recombined here; and
// `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` is the id whose SEMANTICS match Darwin's `CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW`, not the one whose name does
// (see `LinuxClockMonotonic`).
function buildArm64LinuxMonotonicClockChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotTicksPtr = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotFreqPtr = 3 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1 + LinuxTimespecSlots + 1)

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotTicksPtr)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotFreqPtr)

	arm64LinuxReadClockTimespec(asm, clockId: LinuxClockMonotonic)
	arm64LinuxEmitTimespecToNanos(asm)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotTicksPtr)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: 0)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: PosixNanosPerSecond as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotFreqPtr)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: 0)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostMonotonicClockName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxMonotonicClockChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_sleep_ms(x0 = milliseconds)
// ==========================================================================

// Suspend the calling OS THREAD — Windows's `Sleep(ms)`.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS THE OS PRIMITIVE, NOT `sleep(ms)` THE LANGUAGE FEATURE.** `__gt_sleep` parks a GREEN THREAD on
// the scheduler's timer heap and only bottoms out here when there is nothing else to run
// (`GtRuntime.buildGtSleep`), which is why `sleep` and `greenThreads` are separate rows in
// `targetProvidesFacility`.
//
// ⭐ **`clock_nanosleep` ON THE SAME CLOCK THE DEADLINES ARE COMPUTED FROM, RATHER THAN `nanosleep`.** The
// two are the same call for a RELATIVE request, except that `nanosleep` is defined against `CLOCK_REALTIME`
// — so a settime or a large NTP step during the sleep would move a park the scheduler measured against
// `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`. Naming the clock costs one register and removes that disagreement; the Darwin lane has
// no such choice to make, because its `nanosleep` is already on an unsteppable clock.
//
// ⚠ **A SIGNAL CAN CUT IT SHORT (EINTR) AND THIS DOES NOT RESTART IT** — matching Windows's `Sleep`, and
// matching what the one consumer needs: the scheduler re-checks its deadline after every park rather than
// trusting the sleep to have slept (`emitGtParkOnDeadline`). A restart loop here would be a second, weaker
// copy of a check that already exists one tier up.
function buildArm64LinuxSleepMsChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotTsSec = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotTsNsec = 1 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(2)

	arm64PosixMillisToTimespec(asm, ms: Arm64Register.x0, secSlot: SlotTsSec, nsecSlot: SlotTsNsec)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: LinuxClockMonotonic)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxSleepIsRelative)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotTsSec)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: LinuxSleepDiscardsRemainder)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClockNanosleep)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostSleepMsName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxSleepMsChunk'

// `clock_nanosleep`'s `flags`: 0 is a RELATIVE request, `TIMER_ABSTIME` (1) an absolute deadline. The op
// carries a duration, so this is the relative one — and naming it is what stops the 0 reading as "no flags I
// happened to need".
let LinuxSleepIsRelative = 0

// Its `remain` out-param. NULL discards the unslept remainder, which this chunk has no channel to report and
// whose one consumer re-checks its own deadline anyway (see the header).
let LinuxSleepDiscardsRemainder = 0

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE WORKER M's `clone` FLAGS — `pthread_create`'s set, and every bit has a reason.** The child is
// a THREAD of this process, not a process: it must see the same heap the scheduler put the green threads in,
// the same descriptor table the IO ran on, and the same `getpid()` every other part of the runtime reads.
//
//   • `CLONE_VM` — one address space. Without it the M would run the scheduler over a copy-on-write snapshot
//     and publish its work where nobody could see it. It is what makes this a thread at all.
//   • `CLONE_FS` — one filesystem context, so `Directory.setCurrent` on any M is the process's cwd, which is
//     the semantics every other lane's threads have.
//   • `CLONE_FILES` — one descriptor table: a file a green thread opened on one M is readable on the M that
//     resumes it after a park.
//   • `CLONE_SIGHAND` — one signal-handler table. The kernel REQUIRES it with `CLONE_THREAD` (and requires
//     `CLONE_VM` with it), so it is a precondition rather than a preference.
//   • `CLONE_THREAD` — one thread group. `getpid()` then answers the same value on every M (the pipe-name
//     uniquifier and `mrt_host_proc_*` both read it), and the entry stub's `exit_group` at the end of `main`
//     ends the whole program rather than leaving parked Ms behind.
//   • `CLONE_SETTLS` — `TPIDR_EL0` = the slot block this chunk maps for the new M. On this lane the thread
//     pointer IS the per-OS-thread slot base (`mrt_host_tls_alloc`), and `__sched_worker_loop`'s very first
//     act is `tlsSetValue(index, p)` through it — so an M cloned without this would panic in
//     `mrt_linux_tls_set` before it adopted its P.
//   • `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` — the kernel zeroes `PosixWaitOffThread` and wakes it when the thread ends,
//     which IS the join `__sched_stop_workers` performs. See `LinuxFutexWaitShared` for the wake's flavour.
//
// ⚠ **`CLONE_SYSVSEM` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT** though glibc passes it: nothing in this runtime touches a
// System V semaphore, and a flag with no consumer is a claim nobody can check.
//
// ⛔ **THE EXIT SIGNAL IS ZERO — the low byte carries none.** `CLONE_THREAD` forbids one, and a `SIGCHLD` here
// would be the L5 child-process spelling pasted onto a thread.
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED, not transcribed** — a C program on the aarch64 guest issuing `svc` with x8 = 220 directly:
// this exact word, in this exact argument order `(flags, stack, ptid, tls, ctid)`, started a thread that
// read the `tls` page back out of `TPIDR_EL0` (and a slot the parent had written in it), saw the same
// `getpid()` as its parent, took its argument off the stack top, and ended with `exit` leaving the process
// alive — which `exit_group` would not have.
let LinuxCloneVm = 0x00000100
let LinuxCloneFs = 0x00000200
let LinuxCloneFiles = 0x00000400
let LinuxCloneSighand = 0x00000800
let LinuxCloneThread = 0x00010000
let LinuxCloneSetTls = 0x00080000
let LinuxCloneChildClearTid = 0x00200000
let LinuxCloneWorkerFlags = LinuxCloneVm or LinuxCloneFs or LinuxCloneFiles or LinuxCloneSighand or LinuxCloneThread or LinuxCloneSetTls or LinuxCloneChildClearTid

// ⭐⭐ **A WORKER M's OWN STACK: ONE MEGABYTE, WITH A PROT_NONE PAGE UNDER IT.** The size is `CreateThread`'s
// default reserve on the lane this op was shaped for — `osThreadCreate` is `CreateThread(NULL, 0, …)` on
// Windows and 0 means "the image's default", which is 1 MiB — so the three lanes give an M the same order of
// stack rather than each inventing one. It is generous by a factor of about a thousand for what actually
// runs here: an M's stack carries `__sched_worker_loop` and the runtime chunks it calls, and NOT a green
// thread's frames (a switch moves SP to the GT's own stack) nor the syscall shim's (that is the P's own
// `POffSystemStackSP`, a separate 64 KB region). `MAP_ANONYMOUS` commits lazily, so the unused margin costs
// address space and no memory.
//
// ⛔ **THE GUARD PAGE IS NOT DEFENSIVE.** Without it an overflow writes into whatever the kernel mapped
// below — silently, with the corruption surfacing somewhere else entirely. `mprotect(PROT_NONE)` turns that
// into a SIGSEGV at the instruction that did it. MEASURED on the guest: a touch of the guarded page ends the
// toucher with signal 11.
let LinuxWorkerStackBytes = 0x100000
let LinuxWorkerStackGuardBytes = LinuxHostObjectPageBytes
let LinuxWorkerRegionBytes = LinuxWorkerStackGuardBytes + LinuxWorkerStackBytes

// ⭐ **THE TWO WORDS THE CHILD READS OFF ITS OWN STACK TOP: the entry point and its argument.** `clone`'s
// child resumes with x0 = 0 and NOTHING ELSE it can rely on — no return address, and a frame pointer still
// aimed at the parent's stack — so the pair cannot be handed over in registers or in this chunk's frame
// slots, both of which belong to the parent. musl's `aarch64/clone.s` solves it the same way, and this is
// that solution: the parent writes them below the stack top, the child loads them through its own SP. 16
// bytes keeps SP 16-aligned, which AAPCS64 requires at the `blr`.
let LinuxWorkerStackSeedBytes = 16

// ⭐ **THE ONE STATE OF THE JOIN WORD AT `PosixWaitOffThread` THAT THIS COMPILER GETS TO CHOOSE.**
// `mrt_host_thread_create` writes it before the `clone` and `mrt_host_wait_one`'s join arm passes it to
// `FUTEX_WAIT` as the value it is waiting to LEAVE. ⚠ **THE EXITED STATE IS NOT A CONSTANT HERE BECAUSE IT IS
// NOT THIS COMPILER'S TO NAME**: `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` writes a 32-bit ZERO, always, so the join's exit test
// is a `cbz` on the word — the instruction that already means "is zero" — rather than a compare against a
// number we would only be re-deriving from the kernel's behaviour.
let LinuxThreadRunning = 1

// What a worker M's own `exit` reports. Nothing reads it — a thread's status is not waitable and the join
// answers `WAIT_OBJECT_0` for every M — and 0 is what a thread that ran to the end of its loop means.
let LinuxWorkerExitStatus = 0

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_wait_new(x0 = kind) -> x0 = the tagged object handle
// ==========================================================================

// Allocate and initialise one wait object. See `PosixWaitOffKind` for the layout and why it is one page.
//
// ⭐ **ALL THREE KINDS REACH THIS LANE NOW, AND THE THIRD IS WHAT THE WORKER-M `clone` ADDED.** An EVENT is
// a P's wake word, a PROCESS is a spawned child (L5) and a THREAD is a worker M — the kind this chunk
// refused for exactly as long as `osThreadCreate` was a runtime abort here. The kind is still CHECKED rather
// than stored blind: a value that is none of the three means the Std tier reached this op with something
// this backend never mints, and a silently-initialised object of an unknown kind surfaces later as a wait
// that never completes.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY ADMITTED KIND NEEDS THE SAME INITIALISATION, WHICH IS NONE BEYOND THE KIND ITSELF.** A child's
// pid, cached status and killed flag are filled by the spawn and the reap, and a thread's join word is
// written by `mrt_host_thread_create` immediately below; the page is zero-filled, and 0 is the right start
// for every one of them.
//
// ⚠ **A FAILED `mmap` IS A PANIC, NOT A SILENT NULL.** Scheduler bring-up cannot continue without its wake
// events, and the alternative is a store through a negative errno several instructions later with nothing to
// attribute it to.
function buildArm64LinuxWaitNewChunk(globalData GlobalDataTable) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotKind = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)

	let mapped = asm.newLabel()
	let kindOk = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotKind)
	arm64LinuxEmitMapAnon(asm, byteSize: LinuxHostObjectPageBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: mapped)
	arm64EmitPanicConstant(asm, globalData: globalData, bytes: LinuxWaitMapFailedMessage)

	asm.placeLabel(mapped)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotKind)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, imm: PosixWaitKindEvent)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.eq, label: kindOk)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, imm: PosixWaitKindProcess)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.eq, label: kindOk)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, imm: PosixWaitKindThread)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.eq, label: kindOk)
	arm64EmitPanicConstant(asm, globalData: globalData, bytes: LinuxWaitKindMessage)

	// The signal word needs no store: `MAP_ANONYMOUS` hands back a zero-filled page, and 0 IS
	// `PosixWaitNotSignalled`.
	asm.placeLabel(kindOk)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: PosixWaitOffKind)
	arm64PosixTagObject(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, scratch: Arm64Register.x1)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostWaitNewName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxWaitNewChunk'

// `x0 = mmap(NULL, byteSize, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_ANONYMOUS|MAP_PRIVATE, -1, 0)` — the one allocation
// shape this lane's host objects have, shared by the wait object, the per-thread slot block and a worker M's
// stack. The parameters are `PosixMmap`'s, the same ones `lowerOsAllocPages` marshals.
//
// ⚠ **THE SIZE IS AN ARGUMENT BECAUSE A WORKER M's STACK IS NOT ONE PAGE.** It was `arm64LinuxEmitMapPage`
// with `LinuxHostObjectPageBytes` baked in while every host object here happened to be exactly one page;
// a second spelling of the six-register `mmap` shuffle for the one caller that wants a megabyte is precisely
// the duplication this file refuses everywhere else.
function arm64LinuxEmitMapAnon(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, byteSize MachineWord)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: MmapKernelChoosesBase)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: byteSize)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: MmapProtReadWrite)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: MmapAnonPrivateLinux)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, value: MmapAnonFd)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x5, value: MmapNoOffset)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallMmap)
end 'arm64LinuxEmitMapAnon'

let LinuxWaitMapFailedMessage = b"panic: the green-thread scheduler could not map a wait object (mmap failed)\n"
let LinuxWaitKindMessage = b"panic: a wait object of a kind arm64-linux does not mint reached mrt_host_wait_new or mrt_host_wait_one -- this lane mints wake events, child processes and worker-M threads, and nothing else is waitable\n"

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_thread_create(x0 = entry, x1 = arg) -> x0 = the tagged object handle | 0
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`CreateThread(NULL, 0, entry, arg, 0, NULL)` ON A LANE WITH NO `pthread_create`.** The Darwin twin
// is one library call plus a wait object; here there is no libc, so everything a thread needs is built by
// hand: its stack, its guard page, its per-OS-thread slot block, the word its joiner will sleep on, and the
// two-word seed that is the only channel the child has to learn what to run.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THIS CHUNK REPLACES A RUNTIME ABORT, AND THE ABORT WAS RIGHT WHEN IT WAS WRITTEN.** L4 refused to
// write a `clone` here on the measurement that nothing reached the op — `ioCompletionShape(linux)` is
// `inlineCompletion` so there is no completion-drain thread, and `SchedRuntime.buildSchedWakeOrSpawn`'s
// worker M had no producer. The second clause expired: `spawn` publishes a GREEN THREAD, the publish doors'
// green arms call `__sched_wake_or_spawn`, and the default processor count is the machine's. The abort is
// what announced it, by name, on the first `spawn` program built for this lane.
//
// ⛔ **`clone`'s CHILD DOES NOT RETURN LIKE A CALL.** It resumes at the instruction after the `svc` with x0 =
// 0, on the stack this chunk chose, with NO return address and with x29 still pointing into the PARENT's
// frame. So the child path may not touch a frame slot (`arm64EmitLoadSlot` addresses off x29, and the parent
// may already have returned), may not fall out of its block, and must end in a syscall rather than a `ret`.
// It reads its two words through its own SP, zeroes x29 so the panic backtrace ends at this thread's
// boundary instead of walking into the parent's stack, and finishes with `exit`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **`exit` (93), NEVER `exit_group` (94).** They are one number apart in the table and a whole process
// apart in meaning — `exit_group` ends every thread — and `CLONE_THREAD` is precisely what makes the wrong
// one lethal here. The L5 child path states the same trap for the same pair of numbers.
//
// ⚠ **THE JOIN WORD IS PUBLISHED BEFORE THE `clone` AND CLEARED BY THE KERNEL.** `CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID`
// zeroes `PosixWaitOffThread` and wakes it when the thread ends; `mrt_host_wait_one`'s THREAD arm is the
// sleeper. Writing `LinuxThreadRunning` after the clone would race the exit of a thread that finished first.
//
// ⚠ **A WORKER M's STACK AND SLOT BLOCK ARE PROCESS-LIFETIME AND ARE NOT RELEASED ON A SUCCESSFUL START**,
// exactly as the wait objects and the P structs are: an exiting thread cannot unmap the stack it is standing
// on, and the ONE joiner (`__sched_stop_workers`) runs on the way out of the process. Every FAILURE arm below
// does release what it took, because a failed spawn is retried by the next enqueue and a leak there is
// per-attempt rather than per-process.
function buildArm64LinuxThreadCreateChunk(globalData GlobalDataTable) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotEntry = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotArg = 1 as StackSize
	let SlotObj = 2 as StackSize
	let SlotJoin = 3 as StackSize
	let SlotRegion = 4 as StackSize
	let SlotStack = 5 as StackSize
	let SlotTls = 6 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(7)

	let stackMapped = asm.newLabel()
	let guarded = asm.newLabel()
	let tlsMapped = asm.newLabel()
	let childPath = asm.newLabel()
	let cloneFailed = asm.newLabel()
	let releaseStack = asm.newLabel()
	let noThread = asm.newLabel()
	let done = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotEntry)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotArg)

	// The M's stack, with its guard page at the BOTTOM of the region — stacks grow down, so the page the
	// deepest frame reaches first is the lowest one.
	arm64LinuxEmitMapAnon(asm, byteSize: LinuxWorkerRegionBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: stackMapped)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: noThread)

	asm.placeLabel(stackMapped)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotRegion)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxWorkerStackGuardBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: MmapProtNone)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallMprotect)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: guarded)
	// ⛔ A PANIC RATHER THAN A SILENT UNGUARDED STACK. `mprotect` on a page this chunk itself just mapped can
	// only fail if the kernel refuses `PROT_NONE` on an anonymous private mapping; carrying on would hand the
	// scheduler an M whose overflow corrupts a neighbour instead of faulting, which is the failure the guard
	// exists to convert.
	arm64EmitPanicConstant(asm, globalData: globalData, bytes: LinuxWorkerGuardFailedMessage)

	// The child's initial SP: the top of the region, less the two words it reads from there.
	asm.placeLabel(guarded)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotRegion)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxWorkerRegionBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, lhs: Arm64Register.x0, rhs: Arm64Register.x1)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x0, imm: (0 - LinuxWorkerStackSeedBytes) as Imm32)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStack)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotEntry)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxWorkerSeedEntryOffset)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotArg)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxWorkerSeedArgOffset)

	// This M's per-OS-thread slot block, which `CLONE_SETTLS` publishes in its `TPIDR_EL0`.
	arm64LinuxEmitMapAnon(asm, byteSize: LinuxHostObjectPageBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: tlsMapped)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: releaseStack)

	asm.placeLabel(tlsMapped)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotTls)

	// The waitable handle, and the join word inside it, published RUNNING before the thread can clear it.
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: PosixWaitKindThread)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostWaitNewName)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotObj)
	arm64PosixUntagHandle(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x0, imm: PosixWaitOffThread as Imm32)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotJoin)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxThreadRunning)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: 0)

	// `clone(flags, stack, ptid, tls, ctid)` — AArch64's argument order, which is NOT x86-64's (that one
	// swaps the last two). Both were NULL at L5, so the disagreement could not bite there; here they are both
	// live and getting them the wrong way round would set the thread pointer to the join word's address.
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: LinuxCloneWorkerFlags)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotStack)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: LinuxCloneNoParentTidOut)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotTls)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x4, slot: SlotJoin)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClone)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: childPath)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lt, label: cloneFailed)

	// PARENT: the tagged handle is the answer, and `__sched_wake_or_spawn` stores it in the P it just claimed.
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotObj)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: done)

	// ⛔ THE CHILD. Frame slots are the parent's; SP is this thread's own. It never falls out of this block.
	asm.placeLabel(childPath)
	arm64EmitMovFromSp(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x2))
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x2, byteOffset: LinuxWorkerSeedEntryOffset)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x2, byteOffset: LinuxWorkerSeedArgOffset)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x29, value: 0)
	arm64EmitBranchLinkReg(asm.code, reg: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x1))
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: LinuxWorkerExitStatus)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallExit)
	arm64EmitBrk(asm.code)

	// The clone failed — `EAGAIN` on a process at its thread limit is the reachable one. Give back everything
	// this call took, in the reverse of the order it took it, and answer the NULL handle `spawnIt` already
	// reads as "no thread started" (it releases the P it claimed for this M).
	asm.placeLabel(cloneFailed)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotObj)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostWaitCloseName)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotTls)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxHostObjectPageBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallMunmap)

	asm.placeLabel(releaseStack)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotRegion)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxWorkerRegionBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallMunmap)

	asm.placeLabel(noThread)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: LinuxNoThreadStarted)

	asm.placeLabel(done)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostThreadCreateName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxThreadCreateChunk'

let LinuxWorkerGuardFailedMessage = b"panic: the green-thread scheduler could not protect a worker M's stack guard page (mprotect failed)\n"

// Where the two seed words sit relative to the child's entry SP — the entry point first, its argument
// second. Named because the child reads them from a DIFFERENT function than the parent writes them in, and a
// bare 0 and 8 at two sites is the one fact this pair must agree on.
let LinuxWorkerSeedEntryOffset = 0 as MachineWord
let LinuxWorkerSeedArgOffset = 8 as MachineWord

// `CreateThread`'s NULL — what `SchedRuntime.buildSchedWakeOrSpawn`'s `spawnIt` tests to decide it must
// release the P it claimed. It is the handle value, not an errno: this op reports failure by handing back no
// handle at all, exactly as the Win32 call it stands for does.
let LinuxNoThreadStarted = 0

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_event_set(x0 = object)
// ==========================================================================

// `SetEvent` — mark the auto-reset event signalled and wake one waiter.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE PUBLISH IS A `cas`, NOT A STORE, AND THE RESULT IS DELIBERATELY DISCARDED.** `STLXR` gives the
// store RELEASE ordering, which is what a waiter's acquiring take pairs with on a weakly-ordered ISA — a
// plain `str` would need a barrier beside it. That the compare can FAIL is the correct behaviour and not a
// missed signal: it fails only when the word is already 1, and `SetEvent` on an already-signalled auto-reset
// event is a documented no-op. A counting increment would release TWO waiters for two signals, which is a
// worker M woken for work another M has already taken.
//
// ⚠ **THE WAKE HAPPENS WHETHER OR NOT THE PUBLISH LANDED**, which is what closes the window the Darwin twin
// closes by holding the mutex across both: a waiter that observed 1 and is about to sleep will be told
// `EAGAIN` by the kernel's own compare, and one that is already asleep is woken here.
function buildArm64LinuxEventSetChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotSignal = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)

	arm64PosixUntagHandle(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x0, imm: PosixWaitOffSignal as Imm32)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSignal)

	arm64LinuxEmitCasImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, addrSlot: SlotSignal, expected: PosixWaitNotSignalled, replacement: PosixWaitSignalled)
	arm64LinuxEmitFutex(asm, addrSlot: SlotSignal, operation: LinuxFutexWakePrivate, value: LinuxFutexWakeOne, timeoutSlot: SlotSignal, hasTimeout: false)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostEventSetName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxEventSetChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_wait_one(x0 = object, x1 = timeoutMs) -> x0 = WAIT_OBJECT_0 | WAIT_TIMEOUT
// ==========================================================================

// `WaitForSingleObject`. Three timeouts reach it and each has its own arm, exactly as on the Darwin lane: a
// ZERO is a POLL (`__gt_proc_check` wants the state, not a wait), `INFINITE` re-arms until the event fires,
// and a finite one waits ONCE and re-tests.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TAKE IS THE SAME `cas` THE PUBLISH IS, RUN THE OTHER WAY, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES THE EVENT
// AUTO-RESET.** `CAS(signal, 1 -> 0)` succeeds for exactly ONE waiter, which is the "one waiter released"
// half of the contract, and clears the word, which is the "auto" half. The Darwin twin gets both from
// holding a mutex across the test and the store; here the atomicity is the instruction's.
//
// ⭐ **A CHILD PROCESS TAKES THE THIRD ARM, WHICH IS ONE `bl` (L5).** `mrt_host_proc_await` honours all three
// timeouts, and it is `Arm64PosixRuntime`'s single body — the two lanes disagree about what an EVENT is and
// agree exactly about what a reaped child is.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A WORKER M TAKES THE FOURTH ARM, AND IT IS `pthread_join` SPELLED IN THE KERNEL's OWN TERMS.** The
// join word at `PosixWaitOffThread` holds `LinuxThreadRunning` until the thread ends, at which point the
// kernel zeroes it and wakes it (`CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID`). So the join is the same `while (predicate) sleep`
// shape the event arm has, with the kernel rather than this compiler as the publisher — and with the SHARED
// `FUTEX_WAIT`, because the kernel's wake is shared and a private waiter is never reached by it (measured;
// see `LinuxFutexWaitShared`).
//
// ⚠ **A JOIN IGNORES THE TIMEOUT, exactly as the Darwin twin's does, and the one caller passes `WaitInfinite`
// anyway.** `__sched_stop_workers` is that caller and it waits for ever by construction — the worker has
// already been told to stop. A bounded join has no producer to serve and would be untested machinery.
//
// ⚠ A signalled child is signalled FOR EVER — nothing clears the word — which is why that arm does not go
// through the auto-reset event's consuming take.
//
// ⛔ **A DESCRIPTOR IS NOT WAITABLE, AND SAYING SO LOUDLY IS THE POINT.** Every reachable `osWaitHandle` on
// this lane names an object this file minted, so a descriptor or a NULL means the Std tier reached this op
// with something it never should have. Dereferencing it for a kind field would be a wild load; answering
// `WAIT_TIMEOUT` would be a caller spinning on a handle nothing can ever signal.
function buildArm64LinuxWaitOneChunk(globalData GlobalDataTable) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotSignal = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotTimeout = 1 as StackSize
	let SlotResult = 2 as StackSize
	let SlotTsSec = 3 as StackSize
	let SlotTsNsec = 4 as StackSize
	let SlotObj = 5 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(6)

	let isObject = asm.newLabel()
	let isEvent = asm.newLabel()
	let awaitChild = asm.newLabel()
	let joinThread = asm.newLabel()
	let joinTest = asm.newLabel()
	let joined = asm.newLabel()
	let testSignal = asm.newLabel()
	let waitTimed = asm.newLabel()
	let takeSignal = asm.newLabel()
	let finish = asm.newLabel()

	arm64PosixHandleKind(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, handle: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, imm: PosixHandleKindObject)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.eq, label: isObject)
	arm64EmitPanicConstant(asm, globalData: globalData, bytes: LinuxWaitOnDescriptorMessage)

	asm.placeLabel(isObject)
	arm64PosixUntagHandle(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotTimeout)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotObj)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: PosixWaitOffKind)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, imm: PosixWaitKindEvent)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.eq, label: isEvent)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, imm: PosixWaitKindProcess)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.eq, label: awaitChild)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, imm: PosixWaitKindThread)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.eq, label: joinThread)
	arm64EmitPanicConstant(asm, globalData: globalData, bytes: LinuxWaitKindMessage)

	// joinThread: sleep while the kernel has not yet cleared this M's join word. `SlotSignal` carries the
	// futex ADDRESS here exactly as it does on the event arm, so the one `arm64LinuxEmitFutex` shape serves
	// both.
	asm.placeLabel(joinThread)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotObj)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x0, imm: PosixWaitOffThread as Imm32)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSignal)

	asm.placeLabel(joinTest)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSignal)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, label: joined)
	arm64LinuxEmitFutex(asm, addrSlot: SlotSignal, operation: LinuxFutexWaitShared, value: LinuxThreadRunning, timeoutSlot: SlotTsSec, hasTimeout: false)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: joinTest)

	asm.placeLabel(joined)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: WaitObject0)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotResult)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: finish)

	// awaitChild: the child's own wait, in `mrt_host_proc_await` — which honours all three timeouts the
	// callers use, where the event arm below answers only its own kind of object.
	asm.placeLabel(awaitChild)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotObj)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotTimeout)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostProcAwaitName)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotResult)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: finish)

	asm.placeLabel(isEvent)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotObj)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x0, imm: PosixWaitOffSignal as Imm32)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotSignal)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: WaitTimeout)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotResult)

	asm.placeLabel(testSignal)
	arm64LinuxEmitCasImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, addrSlot: SlotSignal, expected: PosixWaitSignalled, replacement: PosixWaitNotSignalled)
	arm64EmitCbnzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, label: takeSignal)
	// A ZERO timeout is a POLL: the caller wants the state, not a wait. Answer the seeded `WAIT_TIMEOUT`
	// without entering the kernel at all.
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotTimeout)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, label: finish)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: WaitInfinite)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64Register.x1, rhs: Arm64Register.x2)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne, label: waitTimed)
	// Unbounded: sleep while the word is still unsignalled, then re-test. That IS the `while (!signalled)`
	// loop the primitive requires — a futex return is advisory (`EAGAIN`, `EINTR`), and re-testing is the
	// only sound response to one.
	arm64LinuxEmitFutex(asm, addrSlot: SlotSignal, operation: LinuxFutexWaitPrivate, value: PosixWaitNotSignalled, timeoutSlot: SlotTsSec, hasTimeout: false)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: testSignal)

	// waitTimed: ONE bounded wait, then a single re-test — the Darwin twin's shape, and for its reason: the
	// caller owns the deadline and re-arming here would silently double a timeout that expired spuriously.
	asm.placeLabel(waitTimed)
	arm64PosixMillisToTimespec(asm, ms: Arm64Register.x1, secSlot: SlotTsSec, nsecSlot: SlotTsNsec)
	arm64LinuxEmitFutex(asm, addrSlot: SlotSignal, operation: LinuxFutexWaitPrivate, value: PosixWaitNotSignalled, timeoutSlot: SlotTsSec, hasTimeout: true)
	arm64LinuxEmitCasImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, addrSlot: SlotSignal, expected: PosixWaitSignalled, replacement: PosixWaitNotSignalled)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, label: finish)

	asm.placeLabel(takeSignal)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: WaitObject0)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotResult)

	asm.placeLabel(finish)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotResult)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostWaitOneName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxWaitOneChunk'

let LinuxWaitOnDescriptorMessage = b"panic: WaitForSingleObject was reached with a DESCRIPTOR or a null handle on arm64-linux, where only a wake event or a child process is waitable\n"

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_wait_close(x0 = handle)
// ==========================================================================

// `CloseHandle` — the ONE op that closes everything Win32 calls a handle, dispatching on the kind the value
// carries (`Arm64PosixRuntime.PosixHandleKindShift` for why it carries one at all).
//
// ⭐ **A ZERO DESCRIPTOR IS A NO-OP, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING RATHER THAN DEFENSIVE.**
// `SubprocessRuntime.emitSubpCloseSlotHandles` closes every slot field unconditionally; on Windows
// `CloseHandle(0)` is a no-op by the API's own contract, while here fd 0 is STANDARD INPUT and closing it
// would take the program's stdin away. The guard is what keeps that graph's convention true on this lane.
function buildArm64LinuxWaitCloseChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotObj = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)

	let releaseObject = asm.newLabel()
	let unmapObject = asm.newLabel()
	let closed = asm.newLabel()

	arm64PosixHandleKind(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, handle: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64PosixUntagHandle(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, imm: PosixHandleKindObject)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.eq, label: releaseObject)

	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: closed)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClose)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: closed)

	// An OBJECT is one mapped page and nothing else — there are no primitives to destroy first, which is the
	// whole of what a futex buys over a mutex-and-condvar pair.
	//
	// ⚠ **A THREAD OBJECT RELEASES THE PAGE AND NOT THE M's STACK OR SLOT BLOCK**, which are process-lifetime
	// (`buildArm64LinuxThreadCreateChunk` carries why an exiting thread cannot unmap the stack under itself).
	// The only caller that closes one is `mrt_host_thread_create`'s own clone-failure arm, where no thread was
	// ever started and that arm unmaps both itself.
	//
	// ⚠ **A CHILD IS REAPED FIRST (L5).** Without a non-blocking `wait4` an exited child that nobody waited on
	// stays a zombie for the parent's whole life, which a spawn/release loop turns into one per iteration. A
	// child still RUNNING cannot be reaped and stays a zombie until this process exits and `init` inherits it;
	// that is the one case POSIX gives no way to close, and it is what `runDetached` asks for by name.
	asm.placeLabel(releaseObject)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotObj)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: PosixWaitOffKind)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, imm: PosixWaitKindProcess)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne, label: unmapObject)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: PosixReapWithoutBlocking)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostProcReapName)

	asm.placeLabel(unmapObject)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotObj)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxHostObjectPageBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallMunmap)

	asm.placeLabel(closed)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostWaitCloseName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxWaitCloseChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_lock_init(x0 = region) / mrt_linux_lock_enter / mrt_linux_lock_leave
// ==========================================================================

// `InitializeCriticalSection` — a free, unowned, un-entered recursive mutex in the region the caller
// allocated. See `LinuxLockOffFutex` for the three words and for why recursion is not optional.
//
// ⚠ **THE REGION IS NOT ZEROED BY ANYONE ELSE.** Its allocator is the slab, whose pages come from `mmap` and
// ARE zero on first use — but a region reused after a free is not, and relying on the difference is exactly
// the kind of "works today" this compiler's assertions exist to refuse.
function buildArm64LinuxLockInitChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	assertLinuxLockFits()
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxMutexFree)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffFutex)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffOwner)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffDepth)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostLockInitName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxLockInitChunk'

// ⭐ **THE LOCK REGION THE Std TIER ALLOCATES MUST HOLD THIS LANE'S MUTEX.** `osLockInit`'s caller
// slab-allocates `OsLockBytes` and hands the pointer over; the three words below are written into whatever
// it is given. The Std constant is sized for the widest lock across the targets this compiler serves and
// says so, but a COMMENT cannot keep that true — so this is the check, at chunk-build time, on the lane that
// would be corrupted.
function assertLinuxLockFits()
	let needed = (LinuxLockOffDepth as ParsedInt) + SpillSlotBytes
	if OsLockBytes < needed 'tooSmall'
		panic("Arm64LinuxRuntime: `OsLockBytes` is {OsLockBytes} but this lane's recursive futex mutex needs {needed} bytes — `osLockInit` would write the owner and depth words past the region the allocator gave it and corrupt whatever it placed after, with no diagnostic anywhere")
	end 'tooSmall'
end 'assertLinuxLockFits'

// `EnterCriticalSection`. Recursive by the owning THREAD, which is what a Win32 critical section promises
// and what `SchedRuntime`'s run-queue publish path relies on.
//
// ⭐ **THE UNCONTENDED PATH IS ONE `cas` AND NO SYSCALL, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT OF A FUTEX.** The kernel is
// entered only when the word is already held; on this lane, where the high-water mark of active worker Ms is
// ONE (`specs-shv2/builtins-cpu-parallel.md` pins it), the contended path is unreachable and is written for
// correctness rather than for use.
//
// **THE CONTENDED LOOP, AND WHY IT IS THREE COMPARE-AND-SWAPS.** A waiter must mark the word CONTENDED
// before it sleeps, or the releaser will take the fast path and never wake it:
//   1. `0 -> 2` — the lock came free while we were looking; take it, conservatively marked, because we
//      cannot know whether a third thread is already asleep on it.
//   2. `1 -> 2` — it is held with no waiter recorded; record one. The result is ignored: a failure means it
//      is already 2 (fine) or that it just became free (the next trip's step 1 takes it).
//   3. `FUTEX_WAIT(word, 2)` — sleep only while it is still 2, which the KERNEL checks, so the window
//      between step 2 and here cannot lose a wakeup.
function buildArm64LinuxLockEnterChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotRegion = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotTid = 1 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(2)

	let acquire = asm.newLabel()
	let contend = asm.newLabel()
	let owned = asm.newLabel()
	let done = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotRegion)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallGettid)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotTid)

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotRegion)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffOwner)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64Register.x2, rhs: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne, label: acquire)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffDepth)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, base: Arm64Register.x3, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x3, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffDepth)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: done)

	// The futex word is at offset 0 of the region, so the region pointer IS its address.
	asm.placeLabel(acquire)
	arm64LinuxEmitCasImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x5, addrSlot: SlotRegion, expected: LinuxMutexFree, replacement: LinuxMutexHeld)
	arm64EmitCbnzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x5, label: owned)

	asm.placeLabel(contend)
	arm64LinuxEmitCasImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x5, addrSlot: SlotRegion, expected: LinuxMutexFree, replacement: LinuxMutexHeldContended)
	arm64EmitCbnzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x5, label: owned)
	arm64LinuxEmitCasImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x5, addrSlot: SlotRegion, expected: LinuxMutexHeld, replacement: LinuxMutexHeldContended)
	arm64LinuxEmitFutex(asm, addrSlot: SlotRegion, operation: LinuxFutexWaitPrivate, value: LinuxMutexHeldContended, timeoutSlot: SlotRegion, hasTimeout: false)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: contend)

	asm.placeLabel(owned)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotRegion)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotTid)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffOwner)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: LinuxMutexFirstEntry)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffDepth)

	asm.placeLabel(done)
	return asm.finishFunction(LinuxLockEnterName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxLockEnterChunk'

// `LeaveCriticalSection`. A nested leave decrements the depth and releases NOTHING, which is the property
// `SchedRuntime`'s header names in so many words.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE RELEASE IS AN ATOMIC DECREMENT AND THE WORD IT DISPLACED IS THE DECISION.** If it displaced
// `LinuxMutexHeld` there was no waiter and the decrement already left the word free — one instruction, no
// syscall. If it displaced `LinuxMutexHeldContended` the decrement left it at 1, which is not free, so the
// word is stored to 0 and one waiter is woken. Reading the displaced value is what makes those two cases
// distinguishable without a second atomic.
//
// ⚠ **THE OWNER AND DEPTH ARE CLEARED BEFORE THE RELEASE, NOT AFTER.** The instant the futex word goes free
// another thread may take it and write its own tid there; clearing afterwards would erase the new owner's
// claim and let a third thread believe it holds a lock it does not.
function buildArm64LinuxLockLeaveChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotRegion = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(1)

	let release = asm.newLabel()
	let wake = asm.newLabel()
	let done = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotRegion)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffDepth)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, imm: LinuxMutexFirstEntry)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.le, label: release)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: LinuxMutexReleaseStep)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, lhs: Arm64Register.x1, rhs: Arm64Register.x2)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffDepth)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: done)

	asm.placeLabel(release)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxMutexFree)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffOwner)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffDepth)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: LinuxMutexReleaseStep)
	arm64EmitAtomicRmw(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x3), addrReg: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x0), operandReg: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x2), opcode: StdAtomicRmwOpcode.add)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, imm: LinuxMutexHeld)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne, label: wake)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: done)

	asm.placeLabel(wake)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotRegion)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxMutexFree)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: LinuxLockOffFutex)
	arm64LinuxEmitFutex(asm, addrSlot: SlotRegion, operation: LinuxFutexWakePrivate, value: LinuxFutexWakeOne, timeoutSlot: SlotRegion, hasTimeout: false)

	asm.placeLabel(done)
	return asm.finishFunction(LinuxLockLeaveName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxLockLeaveChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_tls_alloc() -> x0 = the per-OS-thread slot key
// ==========================================================================

// `TlsAlloc()` — reserve one per-OS-thread slot and answer its index. On the first call from a given OS
// thread it also MAPS that thread's slot block and publishes its base in `TPIDR_EL0`, because on this lane
// there is no libc to have done so (see `LinuxHostObjectPageBytes`).
//
// ⚠ **THE KEY COUNTER IS PROCESS-WIDE WHILE THE BLOCK IS PER-THREAD**, and that is the same arrangement
// `pthread_key_create` has: a key names the same offset in every thread's block. Its `sealedBeforeWorkers`
// class is what makes the plain read-modify-write below sound — `__sched_init_procs` is the only caller and
// it runs during bring-up, before any second thread can exist.
//
// ⭐ **KEY 0 IS REFUSED, WHICH IS THE DARWIN CHUNK'S SECOND ASSERTION AND THE SAME REASON.** With a base of
// 0, key 0 would publish a slot offset of 0, which every reader treats as "the scheduler has not started" —
// a scheduler that silently never runs, with no crash to find it by. The counter pre-increments so the
// first key is 1; this is the check that says so out loud rather than a comment claiming it.
function buildArm64LinuxTlsAllocChunk(globalData GlobalDataTable) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	let mapped = asm.newLabel()
	let haveBlock = asm.newLabel()
	let keyOk = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitThreadPointer(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x0), source: Arm64ThreadPointerSource.linuxWritable)
	arm64EmitCbnzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: haveBlock)
	arm64LinuxEmitMapAnon(asm, byteSize: LinuxHostObjectPageBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: mapped)
	arm64EmitPanicConstant(asm, globalData: globalData, bytes: LinuxTlsMapFailedMessage)

	asm.placeLabel(mapped)
	arm64EmitSetThreadPointer(asm.code, src: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x0))

	asm.placeLabel(haveBlock)
	arm64LinuxEmitDataAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x16, label: LinuxTlsNextKeyLabel)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x16, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x1, imm: LinuxTlsFirstKey as Imm32)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, imm: linuxTlsSlotCount() as Imm32)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lo, label: keyOk)
	arm64EmitPanicConstant(asm, globalData: globalData, bytes: LinuxTlsExhaustedMessage)

	asm.placeLabel(keyOk)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x16, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, src: Arm64Register.x1)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostTlsAllocName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxTlsAllocChunk'

let LinuxTlsMapFailedMessage = b"panic: the green-thread scheduler could not map this OS thread's slot block (mmap failed)\n"
let LinuxTlsExhaustedMessage = b"panic: the green-thread scheduler asked for more per-OS-thread slots than one page holds\n"

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_linux_tls_set(x0 = key, x1 = value)
// ==========================================================================

// `TlsSetValue(key, value)` — `pthread_setspecific` on the other lane, and there ONE import call with no
// chunk at all. It is a chunk here because it owes an assertion that lane does not: `pthread_setspecific`
// works on any thread, while this store needs a BLOCK, and a thread that never called `mrt_host_tls_alloc`
// has `TPIDR_EL0` = 0.
//
// ⛔ **A NULL THREAD POINTER IS A PANIC, NOT A STORE THROUGH ZERO.** Every thread that reaches this call has
// a block — the main thread from `mrt_host_tls_alloc`, a worker M from the `CLONE_SETTLS` in
// `mrt_host_thread_create` — so this is the check that keeps that true rather than assumed, and it names the
// gap instead of writing `key * 8` bytes into the null page. ⚠ It is exactly the check that would have fired
// had the worker M's `clone` been written without `CLONE_SETTLS`: `__sched_worker_loop`'s first act is the
// `tlsSetValue` that adopts its P.
let LinuxTlsNoBlockMessage = b"panic: an OS thread asked to write a scheduler slot without ever having been given a slot block -- on arm64-linux `TPIDR_EL0` is the block base and only `mrt_host_tls_alloc` sets it\n"

function buildArm64LinuxTlsSetChunk(globalData GlobalDataTable) returns FunctionCodeChunk
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(0)

	let haveBlock = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitThreadPointer(asm.code, dest: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x2), source: Arm64ThreadPointerSource.linuxWritable)
	arm64EmitCbnzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, label: haveBlock)
	arm64EmitPanicConstant(asm, globalData: globalData, bytes: LinuxTlsNoBlockMessage)

	asm.placeLabel(haveBlock)
	arm64EmitLslImm(asm.code, rd: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x0), rn: arm64RuntimeReg(Arm64Register.x0), sh: LinuxTlsKeyScaleBits)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, lhs: Arm64Register.x2, rhs: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x1, base: Arm64Register.x2, byteOffset: 0)
	return asm.finishFunction(LinuxTlsSetName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxTlsSetChunk'

// A key scales to a byte offset by the slot width — `key * 8`, spelled as a shift because that is the same
// `index * 8` the Std tier publishes (`SchedRuntime.tlsSlotArrayBase`) and the two must agree exactly.
let LinuxTlsKeyScaleBits = 3 as ShiftCount

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_handle_file_type(x0 = fd) -> x0 = the GetFileType word
// ==========================================================================

// `GetFileType` on a lane whose descriptors carry no such enumeration, and whose image links no libc to ask
// `isatty` with. `ioctl(fd, TCGETS, &termios)` IS what `isatty` is made of: it succeeds exactly on a
// terminal, and the kernel's status becomes the two words the caller compares against
// (`Arm64PosixRuntime.arm64EmitFileTypeFromStatus`).
//
// ⚠ **THE FRAME EXISTS BECAUSE THE KERNEL WRITES THROUGH THE POINTER.** 36 bytes of `struct termios` are
// filled and every one of them is discarded — only the STATUS is the answer — but the buffer must be real
// memory the call may scribble on, which is what a chunk has and an inline `svc` does not.
function buildArm64LinuxHandleFileTypeChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotTermios = 0 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(runtimeFrameSlots(LinuxTermiosBytes))

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxIoctlTcGets as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotTermios)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallIoctl)
	arm64EmitFileTypeFromStatus(asm, terminalWhen: Arm64CondCode.eq)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostHandleFileTypeName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxHandleFileTypeChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// mrt_host_env_read(x0 = namePtr, x1 = bufPtr, x2 = bufLen) -> x0 = bytes written
// ==========================================================================

// `GetEnvironmentVariableA` — copy an environment variable's value into the caller's buffer and answer the
// byte count, NOT counting the NUL.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THERE IS NO `getenv` ON THIS LANE, SO THE VECTOR IS WALKED.** A static image links no libc, and the
// kernel hands the environment to `_start` on the stack rather than through any call — so the entry stub
// captures the vector's base (`Arm64Runtime.emitArm64EnvpFromEntryStack`) and this chunk walks it. Each entry
// is a `NAME=VALUE` C string, and a match is the name followed by `=`: comparing only the prefix would make
// `MAXON_MAX` match `MAXON_MAX_PROCS`.
//
// The measure-and-copy tail is the same one the Darwin lane runs — Windows's contract rather than either
// lane's — and lives in `Arm64PosixRuntime.arm64PosixEmitEnvValueCopy`.
function buildArm64LinuxEnvReadChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotBufPtr = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotBufLen = 1 as StackSize
	let SlotValue = 2 as StackSize
	let SlotWritten = 3 as StackSize
	let SlotCursor = 4 as StackSize
	let SlotName = 5 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(6)

	let scan = asm.newLabel()
	let compare = asm.newLabel()
	let nameEnded = asm.newLabel()
	let nextEntry = asm.newLabel()
	let finish = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotName)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotBufPtr)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotBufLen)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: PosixEnvUnset)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotWritten)

	arm64LinuxEmitDataAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x16, label: LinuxEnvpLabel)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, base: Arm64Register.x16, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, label: finish)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotCursor)

	// scan: `x4 = *cursor`, the next `NAME=VALUE` string, or NULL at the vector's terminator.
	asm.placeLabel(scan)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotCursor)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, base: Arm64Register.x3, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x4, label: finish)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x5, slot: SlotName)

	asm.placeLabel(compare)
	arm64EmitLoadByte(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x6, base: Arm64Register.x5)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x6, label: nameEnded)
	arm64EmitLoadByte(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x7, base: Arm64Register.x4)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64Register.x6, rhs: Arm64Register.x7)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne, label: nextEntry)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x5, base: Arm64Register.x5, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, base: Arm64Register.x4, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: compare)

	// The name ran out: this is a match only if the entry's next byte is the separator. `MAXON_MAX` must not
	// match `MAXON_MAX_PROCS`, and a NUL here (an entry that IS the name with no `=`) is not a match either.
	asm.placeLabel(nameEnded)
	arm64EmitLoadByte(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x7, base: Arm64Register.x4)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x7, imm: LinuxEnvSeparatorByte)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ne, label: nextEntry)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, base: Arm64Register.x4, imm: 1)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x4, slot: SlotValue)
	arm64PosixEmitEnvValueCopy(asm, valueSlot: SlotValue, bufPtrSlot: SlotBufPtr, bufLenSlot: SlotBufLen, writtenSlot: SlotWritten, finish: finish)

	asm.placeLabel(nextEntry)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotCursor)
	arm64EmitAddRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, base: Arm64Register.x3, imm: SpillSlotBytes as Imm32)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotCursor)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: scan)

	asm.placeLabel(finish)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotWritten)
	return asm.finishFunction(HostEnvReadName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxEnvReadChunk'

// ==========================================================================
// L5 — mrt_host_spawn(x0 = argv, x1 = cwd, x2 = creationFlags, x3 = startupInfo, x4 = processInformation,
//                     x5 = lpEnvironment)
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐⭐ `CreateProcessA` ON A LANE WITH NO SPAWN PRIMITIVE AT ALL. The Darwin twin is `posix_spawnp` plus a
// file-actions builder; ⛔ **THERE IS NO `posix_spawn` SYSCALL ON LINUX — it is a libc FUNCTION, and this
// image links no libc.** So the child is built by hand, and AArch64 dropped the legacy spelling of every
// call it needs: no `fork`, no `pipe`, no `dup2`. It is `clone`, `pipe2` and `dup3`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`clone(SIGCHLD)` WITH A NULL STACK AND NO `CLONE_VM` — WHICH IS `fork`, EXACTLY.** The child gets its
// own copy-on-write address space, so everything it does between the clone and the exec — three `dup3`s, a
// `chdir`, a write down the status pipe — happens where the parent's heap cannot be touched. A `CLONE_VM`
// (vfork) child would be sharing the parent's memory with a green-thread scheduler still live in it, to save
// a page-table copy on a path that is about to `execve`.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE STATUS PIPE IS WHAT MAKES `hProcess == 0` REACHABLE, AND WITHOUT IT THIS OP CANNOT KEEP ITS OWN
// CONTRACT.** `posix_spawnp` reports a failed exec to the CALLER; a hand-built fork cannot, because by the
// time `execve` fails the failure is in another process. So a `pipe2(O_CLOEXEC)` is opened before the clone
// and the parent READS it: a successful exec closes the child's end and the read sees EOF, while a failed one
// writes the `-errno` down it. Without this the Std tier's own spawn-failure test — a zero `hProcess`, which
// `Subprocess.run` turns into `SubprocessError.spawnFailed` — could never fire on this lane, and a
// `Subprocess.run(Executable.path("/nope"))` that throws on the other two targets would answer 127 here.
//
// ⚠ **THE PARENT's READ BLOCKS ITS M UNTIL THE CHILD EXECS**, which is the same property the streaming line
// read and the infinite child await already have on both POSIX lanes, and it is bounded by an `execve` rather
// than by anything a program can make slow.
//
// ⚠ **A SECOND SPAWN CAN NOW INTERLEAVE WITH THIS ONE, AND `O_CLOEXEC` IS WHAT MAKES THAT HARMLESS.** This
// paragraph used to rest on *"this lane runs a single active M (`osThreadCreate` is a runtime abort)"*, and
// the worker-M `clone` falsified it — two Ms can be inside this chunk at once. The guard it already named as
// the fallback is the one that carries it: BOTH ends of the status pipe are `O_CLOEXEC`, so a concurrent
// child's own `execve` drops its inherited copy and the reading parent still sees EOF. A runtime chunk still
// parks no green thread, so neither M's sequence is interrupted midway.
//
// ⚠ **PATH RESOLUTION IS THE Std TIER's AND IS NOT REDONE HERE.** `execve` takes a path where
// `posix_spawnp` searches `PATH`, and that search already happened one tier up: `Executable.name(n)` goes
// through `__gt_subp_resolve_path`, the target-neutral `PATH` walk every lane runs, and every other door
// hands over an absolute path (`__gt_process_run` builds `{"/bin/sh", "-c", line}`). ⇒ the Darwin lane's
// `posix_spawnp` performs a SECOND, redundant walk; this one performs none, and a bare name that the Std
// walk could not resolve fails here exactly as an unresolvable absolute path does there.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A CALLER-BUILT ENVIRONMENT BLOCK IS REFUSED HERE EXACTLY AS THE DARWIN TWIN REFUSES IT, AND THE
// SIXTH ARGUMENT EXISTS SO THAT IT CAN BE.** `lpEnvironment` is a NUL-separated BLOCK and `execve`'s third
// argument is a POINTER VECTOR; the conversion is code neither POSIX lane has. It is not free on this lane
// either, which is the thing `execve takes envp directly` makes it look like: the vector would have to be
// built where it can be handed over, and that is the child, between the `clone` and the `execve`, where
// nothing may allocate and nothing may lock — leaving an unbounded stack-built vector on a green thread's
// 2 KB seed, which is the one measurement this chunk's own stack-depth note asks not to guess at.
//
// ⇒ **A NAMED SPAWN FAILURE IS THE ANSWER, AND IT IS STRICTLY BETTER THAN WHAT WAS HERE.** Before the
// operand existed this chunk took five arguments and the block was simply not passed, so an
// `Environment.custom` child on this lane ran with the PARENT's environment while its caller was told it had
// another — the silent wrong answer `StdDialect.osProcessSpawn`'s note says the design exists to prevent.
// A zero `hProcess` plus `ENOTSUP` is the Std tier's own spawn-failure test, and the two POSIX lanes now
// answer one program one way.
//
// ⚠ **AN ORDINARY DESCRIPTOR THE PARENT HOLDS IS INHERITED, WHERE `POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT` WOULD CLOSE
// IT.** What matters is that no PIPE end leaks — an inherited write end is an EOF that never arrives — and
// none can: every pipe on this lane is `pipe2(O_CLOEXEC)` and the three the child keeps are the three `dup3`
// clears the bit on. An inherited data file changes no observable behaviour, which is why the difference is
// recorded rather than bought back with a close-everything loop over the descriptor table.
function buildArm64LinuxSpawnChunk() returns FunctionCodeChunk
	let SlotArgv = 0 as StackSize
	let SlotCwd = 1 as StackSize
	let SlotFlags = 2 as StackSize
	let SlotStartup = 3 as StackSize
	let SlotProcInfo = 4 as StackSize
	let SlotPid = 5 as StackSize
	let SlotHandle = 6 as StackSize
	let SlotStatusFds = 7 as StackSize
	let SlotStatusRead = 8 as StackSize
	let SlotStatusWrite = 9 as StackSize
	let SlotStatusWord = 10 as StackSize
	let SlotStatusCount = 11 as StackSize
	var asm = Arm64RuntimeAsm.beginFunction(12)

	let piped = asm.newLabel()
	let cloneFailed = asm.newLabel()
	let spawnFailed = asm.newLabel()
	let callerBuiltEnv = asm.newLabel()
	let childPath = asm.newLabel()
	let spawned = asm.newLabel()
	let published = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotArgv)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotCwd)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotFlags)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotStartup)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x4, slot: SlotProcInfo)

	// ⛔ **THE REFUSAL IS TESTED FIRST, BEFORE THE PIPE, FOR THE DARWIN TWIN'S REASON**: the arm it takes owns
	// nothing, so there is nothing to unwind on it. `x5` is `lpEnvironment` and is read here and nowhere else,
	// so it needs no slot of its own — every later use of the environment on this lane is the parent's own
	// vector, which the guard has just proved is what was asked for.
	arm64EmitCbnzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x5, label: callerBuiltEnv)

	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusFds)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxOpenCloseOnExec)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallPipe2)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.ge, label: piped)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: spawnFailed)

	asm.placeLabel(piped)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, slot: SlotStatusFds)
	arm64EmitLoadWord32At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x4, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusRead)
	arm64EmitLoadWord32At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x4, byteOffset: PosixPipeWriteEndOffset)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusWrite)

	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: LinuxCloneExitSignalChild)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: LinuxCloneParentStack)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: LinuxCloneNoParentTidOut)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: LinuxCloneNoThreadPointer)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, value: LinuxCloneNoChildTidOut)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClone)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeImm(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, imm: 0)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.lt, label: cloneFailed)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: childPath)

	// PARENT. Drop the write end so the read below can see EOF, then take the child's verdict.
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotPid)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusWrite)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClose)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusRead)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotStatusWord)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: SpillSlotBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallRead)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusCount)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusRead)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClose)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusCount)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: spawned)

	// The child could not exec. Reap it — it is already exiting, so this cannot block — publish the reason it
	// sent, and answer the null handle that IS this op's failure report.
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotPid)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotStatusFds)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: LinuxReapUntilItExits)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: PosixReapDiscardsRusage)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallWait4)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusWord)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: spawnFailed)

	// The clone itself failed: close both ends of a pipe no child will ever hold, then report as above. The
	// reason has to be carried across the two closes, each of which writes x0.
	asm.placeLabel(cloneFailed)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusWord)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusRead)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClose)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusWrite)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallClose)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusWord)

	// spawnFailed: x0 holds the `-errno` of whichever call failed. Publish it where `mrt_host_last_error`
	// reads it and leave a ZERO `hProcess`, which is the Std tier's own spawn-failure test.
	asm.placeLabel(spawnFailed)
	arm64LinuxEmitStashErrno(asm)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: 0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: 0)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: published)

	// The caller-built-environment refusal joins the failure arm at its publishing point: the errno word says
	// why, and a ZERO `hProcess` is the Std tier's own spawn-failure test. Reached only from the guard at the
	// top of this chunk, so it holds no pipe and no child to clean up.
	asm.placeLabel(callerBuiltEnv)
	arm64LinuxEmitPublishErrno(asm, value: LinuxErrnoNotSupported)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: 0)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: 0)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: published)

	asm.placeLabel(spawned)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: PosixWaitKindProcess)
	arm64EmitBranchLink(asm.code, callFixups: asm.callFixups, targetName: HostWaitNewName)
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotHandle)
	arm64PosixUntagHandle(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x3, slot: SlotPid)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x3, base: Arm64Register.x0, byteOffset: PosixWaitOffChildPid)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotHandle)
	arm64EmitBranchTo(asm, label: published)

	// ⛔ THE CHILD. From here to the `execve` nothing but raw syscalls runs — no allocation, no lock, no `bl`
	// into anything the scheduler owns — which is the whole of what "async-signal-safe between fork and exec"
	// asks for. It never falls out of this block: a successful `execve` does not return, and a failed one ends
	// in `exit`, which is why `published` below can sit under it.
	asm.placeLabel(childPath)
	emitArm64LinuxChildDup3(asm, startupSlot: SlotStartup, handleOffset: StartupInfoHStdInputOffset as MachineWord, childDescriptor: PosixChildStdinFd)
	emitArm64LinuxChildDup3(asm, startupSlot: SlotStartup, handleOffset: StartupInfoHStdOutputOffset as MachineWord, childDescriptor: PosixChildStdoutFd)
	emitArm64LinuxChildDup3(asm, startupSlot: SlotStartup, handleOffset: StartupInfoHStdErrorOffset as MachineWord, childDescriptor: PosixChildStderrFd)

	// `DETACHED_PROCESS` ⇒ `setsid()`, which is what `POSIX_SPAWN_SETSID` asks the other lane's spawnattr for:
	// a detached child gets its own SESSION and outlives the parent's terminal.
	let noSession = asm.newLabel()
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: SlotFlags)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x3, value: DetachedProcess as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitRuntimeRrr(asm, opcode: Arm64OpAnd, dest: Arm64Register.x4, lhs: Arm64Register.x2, rhs: Arm64Register.x3)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x4, label: noSession)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallSetsid)

	// A NULL `lpCurrentDirectory` inherits the parent's, which is the absence of a `chdir`.
	let inheritCwd = asm.newLabel()
	asm.placeLabel(noSession)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotCwd)
	arm64EmitCbzTo(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, label: inheritCwd)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallChdir)

	// `execve(argv[0], argv, envp)`. The environment is the vector the entry stub captured — this is
	// `lpEnvironment = NULL`'s meaning, the parent's environment, and the same one `mrt_host_env_read` walks
	// and `StdToArm64Conversion.lowerOsEnvBlock` answers. The guard at the top of this chunk is what makes
	// "NULL" the only `lpEnvironment` that can reach here.
	asm.placeLabel(inheritCwd)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotArgv)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64LinuxEmitDataAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x16, label: LinuxEnvpLabel)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x16, byteOffset: 0)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallExecve)

	// The exec failed. Send the parent the `-errno` the kernel just answered and stop.
	//
	// ⛔ **`exit` (93) AND NOT `exit_group` (94).** They are one call apart in the table and a world apart in
	// meaning: `exit_group` ends every thread of the process, and a child that shares nothing with its parent
	// still shares that call's NAME with the one the entry stub uses to end the WHOLE program.
	arm64EmitStoreSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusWord)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x0, slot: SlotStatusWrite)
	arm64EmitSlotAddr(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotStatusWord)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: SpillSlotBytes as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallWrite)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, value: LinuxExecFailedExitCode)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallExit)
	arm64EmitBrk(asm.code)

	// published: x2 = hProcess, x3 = dwProcessId. Both arms arrive with the pair already chosen, so the three
	// stores that fill the record are written once.
	asm.placeLabel(published)
	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x1, slot: SlotProcInfo)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: ProcInfoHProcessOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x4, value: PosixNoThreadHandle)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x4, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: ProcInfoHThreadOffset as MachineWord)
	arm64EmitStoreWord64At(asm, src: Arm64Register.x3, base: Arm64Register.x1, byteOffset: ProcInfoDwProcessIdOffset as MachineWord)

	return asm.finishFunction(HostSpawnName)
end 'buildArm64LinuxSpawnChunk'

// `dup3(startup->handle, childDescriptor, 0)` — one of the three the child above emits, and the whole of what
// `posix_spawn_file_actions_adddup2` does on the other lane. A zero `flags` is what CLEARS close-on-exec on
// the target, which is how the three ends the child keeps survive an `execve` that closes every other one.
//
// ⛔⛔ **`dup3` REFUSES `oldfd == newfd` WHERE `dup2` RETURNS IT UNCHANGED (`EINVAL`), SO THE GUARD IS
// REQUIRED AND NOT DEFENSIVE.** That case is not exotic: a stream whose stdio kind is `inherit` reaches this
// record as the parent's own 0, 1 or 2, so a plain `Subprocess.run` with no redirection hits it three times.
//
// ⚠ The handle must be UNTAGGED: a pipe end reaches the record as a tagged handle, while an inherited
// standard stream and a redirected file reach it as bare descriptors.
function emitArm64LinuxChildDup3(asm Arm64RuntimeAsm, startupSlot StackSize, handleOffset MachineWord, childDescriptor MachineWord)
	let alreadyThere = asm.newLabel()

	arm64EmitLoadSlot(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2, slot: startupSlot)
	arm64EmitLoadWord64At(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, base: Arm64Register.x2, byteOffset: handleOffset)
	arm64PosixUntagHandle(asm, reg: Arm64Register.x2)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x1, value: childDescriptor)
	arm64EmitCmpRuntimeReg(asm, lhs: Arm64Register.x2, rhs: Arm64Register.x1)
	arm64EmitCondBranchTo(asm, cond: Arm64CondCode.eq, label: alreadyThere)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeReg(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x0, src: Arm64Register.x2)
	arm64EmitMovRuntimeImm(asm, dest: Arm64Register.x2, value: LinuxDup3NoFlags)
	arm64EmitSyscall(asm.code, number: Arm64LinuxSyscallDup3)

	asm.placeLabel(alreadyThere)
end 'emitArm64LinuxChildDup3'

// ⭐⭐ **`clone`'s FIVE ARGUMENTS, AND FOUR OF THEM ARE THE ABSENCE OF A THREAD.** The flag word's low byte is
// the signal the child sends its parent when it dies, and `SIGCHLD` alone — with none of `CLONE_VM`,
// `CLONE_FS`, `CLONE_FILES`, `CLONE_SIGHAND` or `CLONE_THREAD` — is precisely `fork`. A NULL stack means the
// child keeps the parent's SP over a copy-on-write mapping, which is what makes returning from the `svc` in
// two processes sound.
//
// ⚠ **AArch64 ORDERS THE LAST TWO `(tls, ctid)` WHERE x86-64 ORDERS THEM `(ctid, tls)`.** Both are NULL here,
// so the disagreement cannot bite — but a future thread `clone` on this lane must read the arch's own header
// and not x86-64's.
let LinuxCloneExitSignalChild = 17
let LinuxCloneParentStack = 0
let LinuxCloneNoParentTidOut = 0
let LinuxCloneNoThreadPointer = 0
let LinuxCloneNoChildTidOut = 0

// `wait4`'s options word. The reap of a child whose exec failed is the one place this file waits without
// `WNOHANG`, and it cannot hang — the child has already written the pipe and called `exit`. The `rusage`
// argument beside it is `Arm64PosixRuntime.PosixReapDiscardsRusage`.
let LinuxReapUntilItExits = 0

// `dup3`'s flag word. 0 leaves the new descriptor WITHOUT close-on-exec, which is the point: these three are
// the only descriptors that must survive the exec.
let LinuxDup3NoFlags = 0

// `O_CLOEXEC`. Every pipe this lane opens carries it, because there is no `POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT` here
// to invert the inheritance default for the whole exec — see `Arm64PosixRuntime.buildArm64PosixPipeChunk`.
let LinuxOpenCloseOnExec = 0o2000000

// What a child that could not `execve` exits with — the shell's own "command found but could not be run",
// and what `execvp` failing inside `system()` reports. The parent does not read it: the status pipe carries
// the real reason, and this is what a program that never asked would see.
let LinuxExecFailedExitCode = 127

// ==========================================================================
// Assembly
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐ **THE arm64-LINUX HOST SURFACE, GATED EXACTLY AS THE DARWIN ONE IS.** The bits are the Std-tier
// installers' own, so a chunk can never be absent for a body that calls it: one bit, two tiers. The gates are
// DERIVED from which Std bodies emit which ops rather than from which family a chunk's name reads like, which
// is why two of them are unions — and why the `FindFirstFileA` cluster is gated as ONE UNIT: its members call
// each other by `bl`, so a gate that admitted one and not another would be a `CallFixup` naming a symbol
// nothing laid out.
//
// ⚠ **`usesIocp` NOW APPEARS IN FIVE GATES HERE, AND IT NAMED NONE OF THEM UNTIL L5.** This paragraph read
// *"`targetProvidesFacility` denies `subprocess` on this lane, so the three ops the Darwin gates widen for —
// the overlapped read, the child's stdin write, the `PATH` walk's attribute probe — have no producer in a
// program this backend will compile"*, and every clause of it is now false: the facility is provided, the bit
// asks *"does this module read a pipe?"*, and this lane opens one. It is the second sentence of that kind in
// this header (`greenThreads` was the first, at L4), which is the shape a lane completing a table has.
export function appendArm64LinuxRuntimeChunks(chunks FunctionCodeChunkArray, usage RuntimeUsage, globalData GlobalDataTable)
	assertLinuxSearchGeometry()

	let usesFilesystem = usage.usesManagedFile or usage.usesManagedDirectory

	// ⭐ **L3 — FOUR CHUNKS ON FOUR INDEPENDENT BITS, EACH THE Std TIER'S OWN.** `installClockRuntime` builds
	// `__clock_now_unix_s` under `usesWallClock` and `__thread_cpu_ticks` under `usesThreadCpuTicks`;
	// `installProcessRuntime` builds `__proc_exe_path` under `usesExecutablePath`; `CpuParallelRuntime`
	// builds `__cpu_count` under `usesCpuCount`. One bit, two tiers, exactly as the filesystem gates below —
	// so a `bl` into an absent chunk is unreachable rather than merely unlikely.
	//
	// ⚠ **`osGetPid` NEEDS NO CHUNK AND SO NEEDS NO BIT**: it is one `svc` with no out-param, lowered inline
	// (`StdToArm64Conversion.lowerOsGetPid`).
	if usage.usesWallClock 'wallClock'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxWallClockChunk())
	end 'wallClock'

	if usage.usesThreadCpuTicks 'threadCpu'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxThreadCpuChunk())
	end 'threadCpu'

	if usage.usesExecutablePath 'executablePath'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxExePathChunk(globalData))
	end 'executablePath'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE PROCESSOR COUNT RIDES A UNION, AND THE OLD NOTE ABOVE PREDICTED EXACTLY WHY.** It said the
	// compiler-inserted `osCpuCount` in `SchedRuntime.emitResolveMaxProcs` "cannot reach this lane behind
	// `usesCpuCount`'s back … Were that to change, the failure is the LOUD one". L4 changed it and the failure
	// WAS the loud one, on the first green-thread program built for this target: `arm64ResolveCallFixups: bl
	// to unknown function 'mrt_host_cpu_count'`. Every green-thread program reads `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` and clamps
	// it against the machine, so `usesGt` is a second producer of this op — the same shape the read chunk's
	// union below has, and for the same reason.
	if usage.usesCpuCount or usage.usesGt 'cpuCount'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxCpuCountChunk())
	end 'cpuCount'

	// The terminal test, on `installTerminalRuntime`'s own bit — the detecting body it builds under exactly
	// this condition is the only producer of `osHandleFileType`.
	if usage.usesTerminalDetection 'terminalDetection'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxHandleFileTypeChunk())
	end 'terminalDetection'

	// The environment read rides the UNION of `osEnvRead`'s two producers rather than the scheduler's bit
	// (`posixReadsEnvironment`), because a terminal-detecting program need not have a scheduler.
	if posixReadsEnvironment(usage) 'envRead'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxEnvReadChunk())
	end 'envRead'

	// ⭐ **L4 — THE GREEN-THREAD FLOOR, ON ONE BIT.** `usesGt` is the bit `installGtRuntime` itself was gated
	// on, so every body that can `bl` one of these is built under exactly the same condition — one bit, two
	// tiers, which is what makes a `bl` into an absent chunk unreachable rather than merely unlikely. They are
	// NOT split finer: the P's wake event, the run-queue lock, the per-thread slot, the monotonic clock, the
	// OS-thread park and the `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` read are all reached from `__sched_init_procs` and `__gt_init`,
	// which a green-thread program always installs together.
	if usage.usesGt 'greenThreadFloor'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxMonotonicClockChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxSleepMsChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxWaitNewChunk(globalData))
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxEventSetChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxWaitOneChunk(globalData))
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxWaitCloseChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxLockInitChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxLockEnterChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxLockLeaveChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxThreadCreateChunk(globalData))
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxTlsAllocChunk(globalData))
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxTlsSetChunk(globalData))
		// ⭐⭐ **L5's CHILD-PROCESS CORE RIDES `usesGt`, NOT A SUBPROCESS BIT, BECAUSE `installGtRuntime` BUILDS
		// `__gt_process_run` UNCONDITIONALLY.** Every green-thread program statically contains a spawn, an
		// exit-code read and the netpoll's multi-wait — the child store is merely EMPTY in one that never runs
		// a child — so a narrower gate here is a `bl` to a symbol nothing laid out, which is a link failure
		// rather than a diagnostic. The Darwin lane measured that twice while MAC8 was written.
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixProcReapChunk(PosixHostCall.syscall(Arm64LinuxSyscallWait4)))
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixProcAwaitChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixWaitAnyChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxSpawnChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixProcExitCodeChunk())
	end 'greenThreadFloor'

	// ⭐ **THE PIPE SURFACE AND THE DEADLINE, WHICH REALLY ARE NARROWER.** `usesIocp` is the bit
	// `installGtRuntime` builds the pipe substrate under (`__gt_io_read` and `__gt_subp_yield_read`), and
	// `usesStreaming` implies it — so a program that spawns a child WITHOUT reading from it pays for none of
	// these. `osPipePeek`, `osProcessTerminate` and `osTickCountMs` have producers only inside that band.
	if usage.usesIocp 'childPipes'
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixPipeChunk(PosixHostCall.syscall(Arm64LinuxSyscallPipe2), flags: LinuxOpenCloseOnExec))
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixPipePeekChunk(PosixPollCall.ppollSyscall(Arm64LinuxSyscallPpoll, sigsetBytes: LinuxSigsetBytes)))
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixProcTerminateChunk(PosixHostCall.syscall(Arm64LinuxSyscallKill)))
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixTickMsChunk())
	end 'childPipes'

	// ⚠ **`GetLastError` HAS A PRODUCER OUTSIDE THE FILESYSTEM AS OF L5** — `__gt_subp_yield_read` classifies
	// a failed read with it and every spawn failure publishes through it — and that widening lives inside
	// `arm64LinuxUsesHostSurface`, beside the `.data` word the same bodies stash into.
	if arm64LinuxUsesHostSurface(usage) 'lastError'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxLastErrorChunk())
	end 'lastError'

	if usesFilesystem 'filesystem'
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixBaseNameChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixHasWildcardChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixFillFindDataChunk(arm64LinuxStatLayout()))
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxGlobMatchChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxDirPrefixChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxDirFindFirstChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxDirFindNextChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxDirFindCloseChunk())
	end 'filesystem'

	// ⚠ **THE OPEN HAS A SECOND PRODUCER AS OF L5, AND ITS TWIN ON THE OTHER LANE HAS NO CHUNK TO GATE.**
	// `GtRuntime`'s file-backed stdio arm emits `osFileOpenRead`/`osFileOpenWrite` for a stream redirected to
	// a path, so a subprocess program that touches no `File` still branches here. The Darwin lane needs no
	// such widening because an `open` lowers INLINE there (`lowerOsFileOpenDarwin` carries the vararg reason)
	// — this is a gate list that differs because the two CHUNK lists differ, not because the two Std graphs do.
	if usage.usesManagedFile or usage.usesIocp 'fileOpen'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxFileOpenChunk())
	end 'fileOpen'

	if usage.usesManagedFile 'managedFile'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxFileSizeChunk())
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxPathOpChunk(HostFileDeleteName, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallUnlinkat, thirdArgument: LinuxUnlinkFileFlags))
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxFileRenameChunk())
	end 'managedFile'

	// ⚠ L5: `__gt_subp_write_all` and the collect loop's stdin push emit `StdOp.osWriteFile` on the child's
	// stdin pipe, so the write half rides the same union its read twin does.
	if usage.usesManagedFile or usage.usesIocp 'managedFileWrite'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxTransferChunk(HostWriteFileName, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallWrite))
	end 'managedFileWrite'

	// ⚠ **THE READ CHUNK RIDES A UNION BECAUSE `StdOp.osReadFile` HAS TWO PRODUCERS.** `__mf_read` and
	// `__con_read_stdin` emit the identical op — standard input is a descriptor like any other on this
	// platform — so gating it on `usesManagedFile` alone would leave a stdin-only program branching to a
	// symbol nothing emitted, and pushing it under both bits would lay the same symbol out twice.
	if usage.usesManagedFile or usage.usesConsoleStdin or usage.usesIocp 'transferIn'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxTransferChunk(HostReadFileName, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallRead))
	end 'transferIn'

	if usage.usesManagedDirectory 'managedDirectory'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxPathOpChunk(HostDirCreateName, syscallNumber: Arm64LinuxSyscallMkdirat, thirdArgument: PosixDirectoryMode as MachineWord))
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxDirCurrentPathChunk())
	end 'managedDirectory'

	// The mode→attribute-mask decoder is called by BOTH `mrt_host_fill_find_data` (the cluster above) and
	// `mrt_host_file_attributes` (below), so its gate is the union of theirs — which is what lifted it out of
	// the cluster it used to sit inside.
	if usesFilesystem or usage.usesIocp 'attrsFromMode'
		chunks.push(buildArm64PosixAttrsFromModeChunk(arm64LinuxStatLayout()))
	end 'attrsFromMode'

	// ⚠ **`GetFileAttributesA` HAS A SECOND PRODUCER AS OF L5, AND IT IS NOT A DIRECTORY OP.**
	// `__gt_subp_resolve_path` walks `PATH` testing each candidate with `StdOp.osFileAttributes`, so the
	// executable lookup needs this chunk with no `Directory` call anywhere in the program.
	if usage.usesManagedDirectory or usage.usesIocp 'fileAttributes'
		chunks.push(buildArm64LinuxFileAttributesChunk())
	end 'fileAttributes'
end 'appendArm64LinuxRuntimeChunks'
